  :council 2026-07-24 — Measurement accepted (3×-reproduced, null-test-differential design, P0 bit-match discipline) and with it the "router successor NOT de-prioritised; tree half carries measured stakes" reading. Boundary held: EdgeFull is a NAIVE-D4 strawman — its CUBE signal convicts "tree treatment is consequential," never "the principled remedy will also swing." FE(6c) Coatléven pricing remains owed; nothing adopted.
|decision[routing-spiral-landscape-consequence-length-fixed-fan-benign-tree-open] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status council-accepted :topic physics
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  MEASURED — the pricing experiment that `routing-violates-the-potential-identity-and-the-replacement-does-not-fix-it` gates severity on has RUN (`examples/router_pricing`, P0 bit-matches live `erosion::Fluvial::erode` 3×, deterministic 3×, land fraction 88%). Eroded landscape (L19, off-face-centre corner + face-centre D4 control, identical seed/forcing), channel network compared across four routers via the NULL-TEST DIFFERENTIAL (channel-axis-fraction excess over live, corner MINUS centre — the term that isolates the cube-locked part from generic routing change). The spiral's landscape consequence is NON-NULL and REAL, and decomposes into three separately-priced parts: (1) the neighbour-LENGTH correction that shipped this session in `6c1ad97` had a LARGE, clean, cube-locked consequence — +0.10 to +0.20 corner axis de-biasing, ≈0 at the face centre (the textbook null-test signature) — REAL and now REMEDIED; (2) killing diagonals in the drainage FAN is landscape-BENIGN (CUBE −0.02 to −0.04); (3) killing diagonals in the RECEIVER/INCISION TREE is landscape-CONSEQUENTIAL (CUBE +0.07 to +0.26, comparable to the length fix) AND a naive D4 steepest-descent tree is itself NOT cube-safe (it axis-locks harder at the corner, +0.09, and less at centre, −0.16).
  |reason
    The decision's |impact clause named exactly this experiment ("Everything above is the structure of the error, not its landscape consequence. Do not act on severity until that runs.") — it had never run. Built per `#norm-probes-before-claims` / `#norm-probe-sensitivity`: terrain placed OFF face-centre because a D4-symmetric face-centre probe ACQUITS the length bias (demonstrated: its CUBE-locked signal is ≈0 there) — the null-test trap `#obs-routing-curl-spiral` FE(3) warns of, shown to bite this measurement. Baseline arm reproduces the live kernel bit-for-bit (P0), so the router is the ONLY difference between arms; on the first run against a stale pre-`6c1ad97` kernel copy P0 correctly FAILED (max|Δh|≈150 m) and stopped the report. M1 channel-Jaccard (0.27-0.91) and M2 log-drainage Spearman are POOR cube-locked discriminators (large for generic changes and at the face centre both) — the M3 axis-anisotropy null-test differential is the load-bearing metric. The full FE(6b) flux surface (fan + receiver tree, arm `EdgeFull`) is priced, which is what turned a premature "router successor is low-severity" reading into its opposite for the tree half.
    HONEST LIMITS: `EdgeFull`'s D4 receiver tree is a NAIVE diagonal-kill (literal D4 steepest descent) — a strawman for the true corrected router (FE(6c) Coatléven edge-flux FV reconstruction: direction+magnitude from one convictable object). The naive proxy shows the tree treatment is consequential and that a naive kill is not cube-safe; it does NOT measure what the principled remedy would do — FE(6c) is UNPRICED (requires building the reconstruction). One seed / face / level (L19) / synthetic dome; corner = maximum shear (a bound, not a face-average); depression-fill connectivity and talus stay 8-way (diagonal question scoped to the flux surface); metric of record is channel ORIENTATION.
  |impact
    Prices the routing-spiral severity as: length component REAL and FIXED (`6c1ad97`); fan-diagonal component BENIGN; receiver-tree diagonal component CONSEQUENTIAL and UNRESOLVED. So the router-successor question (`#form-grid-equiangular-staggered` FE(4)) is NOT de-prioritised for channel orientation — the flux-TREE diagonal treatment carries real, measured landscape stakes, and the principled Coatléven FV reconstruction (replacing fan AND tree) is what must be priced next, against these stakes; a naive D4 diagonal-kill is not the answer. `#obs-routing-curl-spiral` FE(8) Working Notes "pricing experiment owed" is DISCHARGED for the length + full-diagonal (fan+tree, naive) components; FE(6c) Coatléven-remedy pricing remains owed and is now motivated by measured tree-half stakes. Router successor remains OPEN and UNADOPTED — this entry adopts nothing.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/examples/router_pricing.rs · .super-archive/from-msc/spike-router-pricing/{PREDICTIONS,RESULTS}.md · DECISIONS[routing-violates-the-potential-identity-and-the-replacement-does-not-fix-it] · commit 6c1ad97 (fan-path true lengths) · core/src/obs-routing-curl-spiral.md (FE6, FE8) · #norm-probe-sensitivity · #form-grid-equiangular-staggered (FE4)

|decision[coarse-only-drainage-closure-is-a-nogo] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  SPIKED (`.super-archive/from-msc/spike-nonlocal-closure/`, 8 probes on the real fluvial kernel + verified-land footprints; `PREDICTIONS.md` pre-registered; `RUN.txt`; deterministic, 3× verified). The open half of `#sketch-detail-abstract-reversion` FE(5) — the nonlinear closure for a non-local flux — is **bracketed on both sides**. Positive: the closure's carrier is the drainage **trunk** (a density-vs-flux type error; area-mean is the wrong restriction for a flux). No-go: that trunk is **not recoverable from coarse state** — no pointwise coarse-only closure exists. Claim home for the no-go: `#obs-coarse-only-closure-nogo` (Appendix); positive residue stays on the sketch.
  |reason
    **RE-ANCHOR (measured, incidental but load-bearing).** The wavelet spike's `+5.34 m` commutator was measured on a world that no longer exists: its footprint (ZPos 108544,186368) is now **entirely submarine** (relief 3080–3252 m vs the current derived sea level **5106 m**); erosion there is a near-no-op (max 1.16 m) and the commutator collapses to a fabricated ~0.001 m — the land-guard failure mode. Re-anchored on a fresh 100%-land tile (ZNeg 327680,65536): $\lVert R\circ E - E\circ R\rVert$ signed mean **+7.17 m**, RMS 12.45 m, bias/noise 0.71, against 8.94 m carved. Segments quoting +5.34 need re-anchoring language when this graduates.
    **CARRIER (measured, positive — lives on the sketch).** The drainage graph is the carrier where local statistics are blind. Injecting the fine trunk $\max(A_{\text{fine}})$ per coarse cell **zeroes the bias at depth 2** (+7.17 → **+0.34 m**, bias/noise 0.71 → 0.04 — bias converted to noise, `#norm-bias-vs-noise` success metric). The area-weighted MEAN — the restriction that is exact for the conserved height integral — is robustly WRONG for A: it over-corrects to −4.3…−6.2 m across all tiles/depths, because drainage area is an **accumulated flux, not a density**. This is the cell-side twin of the wavelet spike's face-flux result (fluxes restrict length-weighted so ∫F dl telescopes): A must be coarse-grained as a flux (trunk/through-flow), not as a density (mean). A drainage-commutator proxy predicts the SIGNED height commutator at r = +0.44, where local h-detail gave −0.027.
    **NO-GO (measured — the Appendix claim).** The deployable question is whether the trunk is a coarse-only function of $A_{\text{coarse}}$. It is not. Fit $\max(A_{\text{fine}}) \approx \alpha\,A_{\text{coarse}}^{\beta}$: **$R^2 = 0.06$–0.36 across 2 seeds × 3 tiles × 2 depths** (12 cells; ceiling 0.359). The general-monotone (isotonic) ceiling on the same pairs is 0.401 — the power law was already the ceiling (the earlier "R² bounds every monotone map" argument was backwards and is replaced by this measurement) ⇒ no pointwise coarse-only closure reaches the trunk ON THAT KERNEL. ⚠ SCOPE (2026-07-29 kicking + strengthen attempt): the exclusion is kernel-era-scoped — on current main the same construction measures power-law R² = 0.940 / isotonic 0.982 (probe validated against the spike's own commit, 0.362 ≈ archived 0.359; drift isolated to ~12 commits incl. the craton-nucleation rewrite). The pointwise family is LIVE again on the current kernel; do not quote this entry as present-tense law. Seal deferred. On the very tile it was fit to, the tile-1 fit makes bias/noise WORSE (0.71 → 0.90 — a (fit) row; the earlier "held-out" label here was a mislabel, caught by the 2026-07-29 kicking; genuinely held-out rows are 0.54→0.69, 0.46→0.79, 0.64→0.86 — milder, same conclusion). Mechanism: the coarse flow network REROUTES (it does not merely miscalibrate); the trunk depends on **sub-grid channel POSITION**, which no coarse per-cell quantity encodes. A sub-grid moment is not enough either (sub-grid $A^m$ variance vs gap: r ≈ −0.11).
    **POINTWISE JENSEN (measured, sub-dominant).** FE(4)'s single-epoch decomposition holds with predicted signs ($\langle A^m\rangle - \langle A\rangle^m \lt 0$ concave; $\mathrm{Cov}(A^m,S) \lt 0$), but the +7 m bias is absent at one epoch — it COMPOUNDS over epochs through the drainage feedback (wrong A → wrong incision → wrong topography → worse A), per `#norm-bias-vs-noise` FE(1). Routing, not pointwise concavity, is the dominant mechanism.
  |impact
    NOT a closed door — Joseph: a no-go is an input to the next strengthening round. FE(5) successor text (see `#sketch-detail-abstract-reversion` Working Notes) carries the not-excluded family: a coarse-network-**topology** carrier, a cheap carried **sub-grid channel summary**, or a **fine spine** at adequate resolution — the last now with a measured reason (`#detail-drainage-dependency-planning`: the graph cannot be shortcut with a coarse-state function). Kernel: `Fluvial::{drainage_override, drainage_recalibrate}` are spike-only default-inert instruments (SPIKE INSTRUMENT-tagged, drop-entirely rebase surface, disjoint from `pin_block_means`); neither graduates — the override consumes fine info, the recalibrate is this no-go. UNTESTED: exponents other than $m=0.5,n=1$; deposition/MFD-p regimes; whether a two-variable coarse carrier ($A_{\text{coarse}}$ + coarse slope) or a topology summary raises the 0.36 ceiling.
  |ref .super-archive/from-msc/spike-nonlocal-closure/{PREDICTIONS.md, RUN.txt, src/{main,probes,area,mra}.rs} · #obs-coarse-only-closure-nogo · #sketch-detail-abstract-reversion

|decision[globe-ribbons-are-view-assembly-staleness-not-prior-or-kernel] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic view
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  DIAGNOSED (globe spike + `crates/vivarium-world/examples/coverage_seam_probe.rs` + convicting unit test; reproduced on a fresh L8 6-face store, seed c0fd52ccf3ed2e60). The whole-globe cube-face-edge RIBBONS and edge LAND SPECKLES are a VIEW/ASSEMBLY artifact, NOT a prior discontinuity and NOT cube-locked kernel bias. Root cause: `query::World::load_eroded_regions` selected store roots by the `erosion-tile@` prefix ALONE and ignored the `src=` whole-crate source hash (`#form-complete-content-addressed-key`), so tiles carved under a superseded source tree loaded and blended SILENTLY with current-source prior fallback. That two-datum surface (stale-carved cells vs current prior) steps at coverage boundaries = the ribbons; where the current prior stands higher than the stale carve, edge cells poke above sea = the speckles. This is exactly Joseph's edit->globe loop: ANY `vivarium-world` source edit moves every key's source hash, orphaning the store, and the globe kept painting the orphan as current. The face-seam probe stayed green throughout (fresh store: cross 179 m mean / 2105 max vs within 192 m mean / 2679 max) because it measures cross-FACE prior continuity, blind to the within-face covered<->fallback offset.
  |reason  `#form-sphere-continuous-surface-fields` FE(3) predicted "view assembly paths can re-manufacture cube structure even when h is sphere-honest"; this is the measured instance. `#obs-cube-locked-kernel-bias` FE(5) (mandatory cube control) is DISCHARGED for this artifact: the structure sits at assembly coverage boundaries, not carved by the router. A second, smaller coverage debt is named: `ErodedRegion::grid_pos` reports covered only on `[0, nx-2]`, so the last row/col of every 64-tile falls back to prior even under a matching source hash (1-cell edge seams) — a builder/region tiling gap (`erosion.rs`), separate fix wave (halo/overlap).
  |impact  View-side remedy landed (query.rs is core, globe is spike): `World::eroded_region_census` (fresh/stale/total by source hash) + `World::load_current_eroded_regions` (current-source only). Globe defaults to current-source-only assembly and prints the fresh/stale/prior-fallback census (silent fallback made LOUD, per Joseph's ask); `VIVARIUM_INCLUDE_STALE=1` restores the old silent blend for side-by-side diagnosis. Also retired the stale HUD honesty string ("fluvial on fBm prior / not earned Abyssal land"): the prior IS the tectonic surface (bathymetry + Abyssal isostatic freeboard) since the 2026-07-24 isostasy read; land is the freeboard read — Claimed, not yet Kept. Verified workflow for Joseph: edit `vivarium-world` -> `vivarium build` (rerun; source hash moved, so this recomputes) -> `vivarium-globe`. Skipping the rebuild now shows a loud "N STALE hidden -- rerun: vivarium build" census instead of silent ribbons.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/query.rs (eroded_region_census, load_current_eroded_regions, RegionCensus + convicting test) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/coverage_seam_probe.rs · spikes/globe/src/main.rs (census HUD, honesty line, VIVARIUM_INCLUDE_STALE) · core/src/form-sphere-continuous-surface-fields.md (FE3 + view-assembly staleness status) · #form-builder-admission · #obs-cube-locked-kernel-bias

|decision[mean-pin-retirement-executed-block-const-convicted-deletion-is-the-fix] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session 4d13314a-8a6a-4f42-9ee0-558f4acf5add
  EXECUTION of the council-accepted retirement (`mean-pin-manufactures-the-seam-and-the-mass` :council note fronts it via `code-to-claim-wave-council-fronts-conservation-and-execution` clause 2). Direction (retire-or-replace) was accepted; THIS entry carries the *verdict* on the replacement experiment and what shipped — proposed, not pre-accepted. **Outcome: the block-constant-injection replacement is CONVICTED; DELETION (leaf-only, no operator) is the measured-best executable step; the honest replacement is `#form-face-flux-register` + leaf-only, out of this territory's scope.**
  |context-first
    ⚠ **THE MEASUREMENT BASIS MOVED — the RUN.txt numbers (seam 5.77; zero-physics 1.93/1.10; mass +0.136%) are STALE.** They were measured at footprint (108_544/186_368), which classified as land only against the DEPRECATED `gen::SEA_LEVEL_M = 4000`. Since sea level became *derived* (`sea_level::derived_sea_level_m(0) = 5106 m`) and the prior was resampled (spine v2), that region is ~2 km of deep OCEAN; `seam_ridge` there now reports a fake ~1.70 on no-op submarine terrain (`#norm-probe-sensitivity` seabed-no-op species — and `seam_ridge` has no land guard). The spike (PROBE 0 land guard) was RE-BASED to (135_168/167_936): interior face ZPos, 100% subaerial under derived sea, relief ~370 m, 512-aligned. All numbers below are current-main, seed 0, DETERMINISTIC (bit-identical ×3).
  |reason
    **(1) THE SEAM IS WORSE THAN THE STALE NUMBERS SAID.** On real high-relief land, worst case (macro 80e / fine 150e): pin-ON (bilinear, today) **12.81**, pin-OFF **3.84**. Zero-physics control (both tiers 0 epochs): pin-ON **1.96**, block-const **1.66**, pin-OFF **1.17** — the tile machinery alone still manufactures a ~2× ridge, and mean-pin is the whole of that excess. Removing the pin more than halves the ridge, exactly as the old footprint said, but from a higher base.
    **(2) BLOCK-CONST INJECTION FIXES THE FIDELITY LIE — AND ONLY THAT.** `h ← h + Δ_block` added as a CONSTANT (not bilinearly upsampled) genuinely pins the mean: residual |block mean − target| median **0.00012 m** vs bilinear's **0.489 m** (max 2.929 m). So `#form-rl-closure-algebra` law (1) `R∘L = id on the mean` becomes TRUE under block-const. That is the only defect it repairs.
    **(3) IT DOES NOT FIX THE MASS SOURCE.** ∫h dA before→after pin is IDENTICAL for bilinear and block-const: **+0.0291% / +0.1197% / +0.2172%** (fine 18e/80e/150e). Coercing the fine tier toward the stale, less-eroded coarse target is the mass mechanism; how the delta is distributed within the block is irrelevant to the total. Both fabricate the same rock.
    **(4) IT DOES NOT FIX THE SEAM — IT MOVES IT INWARD (a `#norm-probe-sensitivity` trap).** Block-const's ratio (3.33) reads NOMINALLY below pin-off (3.84), but that is an artifact of the ratio's denominator. Absolute SEAM curvature: pin-off **0.274**, block-const **0.524** (nearly 2×). Block-const's flattering ratio comes entirely from inflating the INSIDE curvature to **0.267** (5× pin-off's 0.055): the per-block constant stamps a discontinuous step at every 4-cell block boundary — a grid-locked interior WASHBOARD. It trades a boundary ridge for an interior washboard; the seam/√(out·in) metric rewards that, the absolute curvatures convict it. (This is the same lesson as injection-alone up-propagate, measured **8.99**, worse than pin-off: injection needs its refluxing partner.)
    **⇒ VERDICT.** Bilinear pin: convicted (doesn't pin, mass source, biggest seam manufacturer). Block-const pin: convicted (pins the mean but keeps the mass source and washboards the interior). Pin-OFF: lowest absolute seam (0.274), lowest interior curvature (0.055), zero manufactured mass, honest ratio (3.84). **Deletion is the executable step now.** The admissible non-deletion fix (FE(8)) is leaf-only evolution + a single-valued `#form-face-flux-register` — NOT this operator, and owned by the cross-face worktree spike.
  |execution
    **`pin_block_means` has NO production caller** — world-gen composition (`query.rs`: `from_surface` + `surface_at`) seeds a fine tier from the coarse surface and never pins. Its only live footprint was (a) the operator's docstring, which asserted it "maintains the coarse tier's average" / `R∘L=id` (the false law), now corrected to name it a convicted, non-production instrument; and (b) the self-certifying test `pin_preserves_parent_means` — a green with a **2 m tolerance "sized to the residual,"** the exact `#norm-probe-sensitivity` species `#obs-mean-pin-manufactures-seam`'s Discussion names. That test is RESTATED to CONVICT (`bilinear_pin_does_not_preserve_means_block_const_does`, hermetic, self-calibrating relative gap: block-const pins to machine precision, bilinear does not by orders of magnitude — cannot be re-tuned into a false green). The convicted `pin_block_means_const` is retained beside `pin_block_means` as the measured candidate the spike exercises; neither is wired into a live surface.
  |impact
    `seam_ridge` stays RED by design (it measures the honesty gap; the real fix is flux-BC tiles). `#obs-mean-pin-manufactures-seam` re-based to current-main numbers + block-const outcome + the no-production-caller finding; `#form-rl-closure-algebra` compliance-debt numbers updated. `gen::SEA_LEVEL_M = 4000` is deprecated and should not be used for land classification anywhere (the spike used it; fixed). RUN.txt regenerated on the re-based footprint.
  |ref msc/spike-wavelet-store/src/probes.rs (PROBE 6/7 — PinMode::{Bilinear,Off,BlockConst}) + RUN.txt · crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (pin_block_means docstring; pin_block_means_const; bilinear_pin_does_not_preserve_means_block_const_does) · crates/vivarium-world/src/query.rs (no pin in composition) · core/src/obs-mean-pin-manufactures-seam.md · core/src/form-rl-closure-algebra.md · #form-face-flux-register · #norm-probe-sensitivity · DECISIONS[mean-pin-manufactures-the-seam-and-the-mass]

|decision[mantle-thermal-nomos-built-and-emergence-runs-in-time] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  BUILT (council-fronted, `mantle-thermal-nomos-ruled-in-scope`) — the head of the accepted freeboard chain stops being a constant. `mantle_thermal.rs` promotes `MANTLE_TP_C` to a declared secular-cooling TRAJECTORY T_p(t) = TP_MODERN + (MANTLE_TP_C − TP_MODERN)·exp((age − present)/τ): monotone in time, through a present-Abyssal anchor (~3.2 Ga, 1550 °C) toward the modern asymptote (1350 °C), clamped [1350, 1650]. Declared crude — physics Low, order-of-magnitude Earth pins, NOT integrated from a heat budget (Korenaga 2017 still unread), no plate history. Registered as the `mantle-thermal` NOMOS producing `mantle potential temperature`, consumed by `lithosphere` (new dep + `MANTLE_POTENTIAL_TEMP` flux quantity + version bump) — the chain head wired into the flux web. `sea_level` gains a memoized epoch chain (`derived_sea_level_at`/`_at_tp`, `tectonic_surface_at_tp`, `emerged_land_record_at_tp`); every seed-only caller stays pinned to present-Abyssal (zero regression, the coherence pin below).
  |reason
    Joseph's ask this session: what stands between the tree and "watching principled land-masses rise from the sea." The column and the pour were already pure in T_p, so the time axis of emergence WAS just this nomos plus a memoized chain over the existing read. Probes first (`#norm-regime-probes`): `mantle_cooling_probe` walks the canonical Abyssal chain and MEASURES (level 7, seeds 0/1/7) three convicting invariants, all green — (1) land fraction MONOTONE non-decreasing as the mantle cools; (2) a near-WATER-WORLD start at the hot early-Abyssal end (0.05/0.02/0.09 % at T_p≈1641); (3) the present-Abyssal epoch IN-BAND and EQUAL to the live seed-only world (4.55/2.06/4.88 % at 1550 — matching the previously recorded 4.5/2.1/4.8 %, the coherence pin, unit-tested exact). The trajectory: seed 0 stays in-band through the cold epochs (→12.8 %); seeds 1 and 7 cross ABOVE the ~20 % early-Earth ceiling at the coldest epochs (27.7 %, 24.0 %) — honest present-truth, those epochs are the world maturing PAST the early-Earth regime toward modern-like freeboard (the "later era" clause the static predicate named not-yet-predicable now runs crudely), not a calibrated Abyssal fraction claim per epoch.
    Perf bug found and fixed en route: `freeboard_m_at_tp` read the UN-memoized `reference_m_at_tp` (a whole-sphere integral) per cell — an O(N²) blow-up that hung the pour. `reference_m_at_tp` is now memoized per (seed, T_p) (the default `reference_m` delegates to it). HONEST NEGATIVE recorded on the segment: cooling does NOT conserve column mass across epochs (the thermal driver rewrites oceanic-crust thickness/density with no melt-flux accounting — the differentiation/rock-mass ledger is open); the within-epoch balance still holds exactly (reference re-integrated per T_p, area-mean freeboard ≡ 0).
  |impact  `mantle_thermal.rs` (5 unit tests) + `flux::MANTLE_POTENTIAL_TEMP` + `MANTLE_THERMAL`/`LITHOSPHERE` NomosDecls + `sea_level` epoch chain + `sea_level::cooling_grows_land_from_a_water_world_through_the_present` (monotone + water-world + coherence-pin conviction); `examples/{mantle_cooling_probe,emerging_land}.rs` (probe + from-space ASCII emergence sequence); `#form-isostasy-column` FE(7)/(8)/status/Working-Notes + OUTLINE geosphere row + ASSUMPTIONS "mantle cooling trajectory" row + ordinum `promise[emerged-land]` predicate note (deep time now runs crudely) updated to present truth. Touched-module tests green (26) and the cooling conviction green (13, 1.6 s). Still Claimed, NOT Kept; the mantle-thermal RATE LAW, water loading, rock-mass return, and wiring the now-approachable temporal predicate clauses into the verdict remain open (FE(8)). Build verdict fresh — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/mantle_thermal.rs · crates/vivarium-world/src/sea_level.rs · crates/vivarium-world/src/nomotheke.rs (MANTLE_THERMAL, LITHOSPHERE) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/mantle_cooling_probe.rs · core/src/form-isostasy-column.md · ASSUMPTIONS.md ("mantle cooling trajectory") · DECISIONS[mantle-thermal-nomos-ruled-in-scope]

|decision[globe-first-light-blocks-on-isostasy-pour-never-block-violation] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic view
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  FINDING (Joseph observed live: globe window hangs indefinitely at "pulling surface from store...", only the sun HUD line drawn). The globe worker computes `sea_level::derived_sea_level_m(seed)` — an L8 pour of ~393k `lithosphere::freeboard_m` (isostasy) evaluations — SYNCHRONOUSLY before it can emit the first frame; today's isostasy read made each `freeboard_m` call costly, so first light can block for minutes, worse under store/CPU contention with a concurrent builder/probe. This violates the never-block law (`#form-builder-admission` FE(4) / `#form-core-view-wall` FE(4) / `#detail-builder-daemon`: explorers work coarse, never block) — but note it is a cold COMPUTE on the critical path, not a store-lock stall.
  |reason  The heavyweight isostasy pour sits on the first-light critical path and is recomputed every launch (memoized per-process only). The pour cost is real and also slows any standalone `derived_sea_level_m` consumer (measured: the L8 pour alone runs into minutes on a loaded machine).
  |impact  VIEW-SIDE HALF LANDED (2026-07-24, `spikes/globe/src/main.rs`): first light now renders on a coarse provisional sea (`provisional_sea_m`, an L4 view-side pour over the pub `tectonic_surface_m`, ~sub-second) while a side thread computes the real L8 `derived_sea_level_m` and self-pokes a rebuild when it lands; the HUD census shows `sea: PROVISIONAL (coarse) -> refining` then `sea: derived L8`. Verified headless: whole run 4.3 s wall on the stale ribbon-world (vs the prior multi-minute hang), first light instant, refine confirmed (final HUD read `derived L8`). STORE-SIDE HALF NOW LANDED (2026-07-24, `DECISIONS[epoch-surfaces-are-store-citizens]`): the per-epoch derived sea + rock-mass-ledger integrals are store citizens (`query::World::epoch_reduction`); `vivarium build` materializes the ladder and the globe warmer / present-refine threads Hit the store, so a warmed process runs no pour at all. `#form-builder-admission` Known-incomplete (5) is closed.
  |ref spikes/globe/src/main.rs (spawn_worker: pre-loop `derived_sea_level_m`) · crates/vivarium-world/src/sea_level.rs (SAMPLE_LEVEL=8 pour) · #form-builder-admission FE(4) · #form-core-view-wall FE(4)

|decision[rock-mass-ledger-built-erosion-returns-mass-rebound-trims-the-amber] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  BUILT (council-fronted, `code-to-claim-wave-council-fronts-conservation-and-execution` clause 1 — conservation debt under `#norm-declared-violation-is-not-license`, not a fidelity rung that waits by being listed). The freeboard chain's LAST-OPEN rung — erosion's mass RETURN to the lithospheric column, with isostatic rebound bounding relief (`#form-isostasy-column` FE(8) → now FE(9)). `erosion_return.rs` closes it at the POUR GRAIN as a pure, fated operator: (1) DEBIT — subaerial columns lose crust by a declared relief-driven law (`crust_eroded_m` = φ·stand-above-sea, φ=1, physics LOW — a stand-in for ∫stream-power where the pour grain has no drainage, sibling to the fated inventory; clamped to the felsic pile); (2) REBOUND — automatic in the Airy read, removing crust δ lowers the stand by only δ(ρ_a−ρ_c)/ρ_a ≈ 17% for felsic crust, the rest rebounds (`lithosphere::surface_drop_per_crust_removed`, unit-tested) — this is why the ledger BOUNDS relief rather than planing it flat; (3) CREDIT — the eroded mass is RETURNED to the column as a uniform submarine sediment blanket (ρ_sed=2500), so basins fill and load-subside while cratons erode-and-rebound (relief compresses from both ends).
  |reason
    `#norm-probes-before-claims` / `#norm-regime-probes`: the conservation claim ships with a probe that can FAIL. CLOSED-BOX CONSERVATION (the failable claim): Σ(crust·ρ_c + sed·ρ_sed)·area is invariant across the pass to float precision — measured rel gap 1.4–1.6e-12, seeds 0/1/7 (`rock_mass_is_conserved_across_the_ledger_pass`). The post-erosion freeboard re-references to zero mean (rise here IS subsidence there, still — `post_erosion_freeboard_is_zero_mean…`). REBOUND is a pure algebraic conviction (`erosional_unloading_rebounds_surface_falls_by_less_than_the_rock_removed`): strip 3 km crust, surface falls <0.25× that. Purity kept: per-cell debit/credit read memoized global integrals, so the post-erosion column is a pure function of (seed, cell, T_p) — a single erosion MATURITY step (iterated epochs are a further rung, like mantle_thermal's discrete epochs).
    CALIBRATION OBSERVATION (measured, level 8, φ=1, `examples/rock_mass_ledger_probe.rs`): the amber over-stand from `emerged-land-record-predicate-built-and-two-clauses-convict` trims TOWARD the band — seed 0 2436→2025 m, seed 7 2386→1982 m (amber→IN-BAND, relief clause PASS), seed 1 1724→1435 m. The ~17% trim EQUALS the felsic rebound fraction (ρ_a−ρ_c)/ρ_a — an internal consistency check, not a tuned target. "Toward, not magically into": seed 0 lands at 2025 m, still a hair over the 2000 m flag — an honest ledger moves peaks, it does not clamp them.
  |impact  NOT Kept — still Claimed. ADOPTED as the live default surface (see :council note below): `sea_level::{tectonic_surface_at_tp, derived_sea_level_at_tp}` repoint to the post-erosion surface, so every downstream reader (pour, land classification, `emerged_land_record`, globe, `gen`, `query`, erosion outlets) sees it; the raw isostatic surface is retained as `sea_level::*_pre_ledger_*`. `LITHO_COLUMN` promise upgraded `NotTracked`→`Conserved` with the closed-box probe as its convicting instrument. LIVE DEFAULT now measures (level 8, seeds 0/1/7): land 4.49/2.05/4.78%, peak stand 2025/1435/1982 m — seed 7 into-band, seed 0 residual ~2025 m amber (v1 crudeness + open strength limit); pre-ledger input was 2436/1724/2386 m. `#form-isostasy-column` FE(9)/FE(6)/FE(8)/FE(2)/status/Working-Notes; `#detail-erosion-composition` residual row; `#form-flux-web` (decl-upgrade-done note); OUTLINE geosphere row; ordinum `promise[emerged-land]` predicate line; ASSUMPTIONS "erosion maturity" + sediment-density rows — all present truth. Code: `lithosphere.rs` (Column.sediment_m + buoyancy term + rebound identity + test), `sea_level.rs` (`pour_ocean_over` factored; public surface/sea repointed post-ledger; `*_pre_ledger_*` retained), `erosion_return.rs` (new module + 4 tests), `nomotheke.rs` (LITHOSPHERE Conserved + version bump), `examples/{rock_mass_ledger_probe,emerged_land_probe}.rs`. `bin/check` green (134 lib tests + cli_admission + determinism clippy; ~10 s — the default pour is now ~4× heavier: pre-pour + ledger + post-pour). OPEN (named, not this adoption): routed/proximity-weighted deposition (v1 uniform blanket); iterated erode→rebound epochs (v1 one φ=1 step); water loading; store-as-save the derived sea at build time (pour now heavier); wiring the ledger as its own flux-web nomos edge. Build verdict fresh — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |council 2026-07-24 — ADOPTION RULED IN (under Joseph's standing grant, recorded by the coordinator): wire the ledger into the default pour as the live post-erosion surface; upgrade `LITHO_COLUMN` NotTracked→Conserved with the closed-box test as its convicting instrument; keep φ=1 single-step for v1 (iterated sub-maturity epochs are a later rung). Reasoning: the ledger is the honest surface (the pre-ledger one over-stands by known-missing physics); the globe peer's path is landed and idle (first-light fix `3c135b7`), so store invalidation is the system working (the stale census tells Joseph to rebuild); and `#norm-declared-violation-is-not-license` cuts against leaving a built conservation fix unwired as a display piece. Entry status stays `proposed` (build verdict fresh); this note is the record that adoption was ruled.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion_return.rs · crates/vivarium-world/src/lithosphere.rs (Column.sediment_m, surface_drop_per_crust_removed) · crates/vivarium-world/src/sea_level.rs (pour_ocean_over, *_pre_ledger_*, repointed default) · crates/vivarium-world/src/nomotheke.rs (LITHOSPHERE Conserved) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/rock_mass_ledger_probe.rs · core/src/form-isostasy-column.md (FE9) · core/src/detail-erosion-composition.md · core/src/form-flux-web.md · tabularium/terrestris.ordinum.udon · ASSUMPTIONS.md · DECISIONS[code-to-claim-wave-council-fronts-conservation-and-execution] · DECISIONS[emerged-land-record-predicate-built-and-two-clauses-convict]

|decision[craton-nucleation-and-deep-time-playback-ruled-in-scope] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status council-accepted :topic physics
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  SCOPE RULING (session grant; Joseph's direct asks). (1) CRATON NUCLEATION-AND-GROWTH: replace the fBm-threshold craton placement — the declared differentiation stand-in whose percolation-speckle morphology Joseph identified on the live globe — with a fated nucleation-and-growth law: few nucleation sites, melt-localized accretion, coherent cratons with a characteristic scale, composing with the cooling trajectory so cratons grow outward over epochs. Still crude/declared (physics Low-Med); the isostasy read and pour are unchanged consumers. This is the horizontal half of the differentiation rate law `#form-isostasy-column` FE(8) leaves open; the vertical half (heights, mass, rebound) landed today. (2) DEEP-TIME PLAYBACK: an epoch-animated globe (ante-mundane-ward history to present) as observe-only pulls over the memoized epoch chain — view work under `#form-core-view-wall`, no new law; the store IS the time machine (`#form-store-as-save` FE(4): Hit is matured state; epochs are keyed memos).
  |reason  (1) A threshold slice through a scale-free field is percolation speckle — the morphology class is wrong even at honest heights; nucleation-growth is the cheapest law with the right class, and it makes the emergence animation read as continents assembling. (2) Playback is structurally free by design: determinism + complete keys mean every epoch is replayable; the only costs are which epochs are materialized (grain), disk (evictable — regenerable by recompute), and a view that iterates epoch keys.
  |impact  Two agents launched: nucleation (lithosphere/mantle territory; timing-clause work re-queued behind it since placement changes the trajectory again); playback (globe/query territory, chain-agnostic — reads whatever epoch memos exist). Not covered: plate tectonics as placement mechanism (separate council decision stands); any claim nucleation is Earth-calibrated.
  |ref core/src/form-isostasy-column.md (FE8) · core/src/form-store-as-save.md · core/src/form-temporal-lod-regimes.md · DECISIONS[mantle-thermal-nomos-ruled-in-scope]

|decision[deep-time-playback-built-the-globe-animates-the-cooling-chain] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic view
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  BUILT (council-fronted, `craton-nucleation-and-deep-time-playback-ruled-in-scope` clause 2 — Joseph's direct ask: "after the difficult simulations are run, watch it animated — the globe evolving to current state"). The Bevy globe (`spikes/globe/src/main.rs`) gains a DEEP-TIME PLAYBACK mode: press T to watch the planet age along the mantle-thermal cooling ladder (`mantle_thermal::abyssal_epochs`) — hot near-water-world (3.60 Ga, T_p 1641 C, ~0.0 % land) through the present-Abyssal anchor to the cooling late-Abyssal (2.60 Ga, T_p 1464 C, ~20.6 % land). K play/pauses the hot->cool sweep (loops), J/L step epochs, and a text timeline shows the ladder with the present-Abyssal epoch marked (coincides with the live world) and per-epoch warm/materializing state. VERIFIED with three scripted autoshots (`VIVARIUM_PLAYBACK`/`VIVARIUM_EPOCH` env, seed f1d242b21d8d89ea): present regression intact, epoch 0 water-world, epoch 5 continents — the emergence reads as land rising from the sea.
  |reason
    Spike-only by construction (`#form-core-view-wall`: a view observes, authors no evolution). The epoch axis is the LAW's ladder — the view SELECTS which materialized epoch to observe, it does not invent a T_p (FE(4) observe-only). The surface at each epoch is the pure isostatic tectonic surface `sea_level::tectonic_surface_at_tp` at that mantle temperature with the epoch's `derived_sea_level_at_tp` waterline — exactly the reads `examples/emerging_land.rs` already walks and `cooling_grows_land_from_a_water_world_through_the_present` convicts; no new law, no core edit. NEVER-BLOCK applied to time (`#form-builder-admission` FE(4)): each epoch's heavy law values (the derived sea, which internally warms the rock-mass-ledger + pour integrals so per-cell reads go memo-cheap) are computed on a BACKGROUND warmer thread, present-first then hot->cool; the render worker is only ever offered an already-warm epoch, and the sweep BUFFERS honestly on an unwarmed next epoch rather than skipping to an uncomputed frame or cold-computing on the build path.
    HONEST BOUNDARIES stated on the HUD and in code: (1) epochs are DISCRETE (the law's ladder) — no cosmetic interpolation, so every drawn frame is a real materialized world-truth epoch, never a confusable-with-truth tween; (2) fluvial eroded tiles are OMITTED from playback (they exist only for the present epoch) — playback shows the pure isostatic epoch surface, and the present epoch coincides exactly with the live world's prior (the `tectonic_surface_at_tp(MANTLE_TP_C) == gen::initial_topography_m` coherence pin) minus those present-only tiles; (3) the round-blob craton morphology visible in playback is the fBm-threshold placement the parallel nucleation peer is replacing — playback is CHAIN-AGNOSTIC and will show coherent continents automatically once nucleation lands, no playback change needed.
  |impact  `spikes/globe/src/main.rs` only (present live view unchanged, byte-identical ghost sampling when `epoch=None`). Store-side epoch-surface memoization (warm the derived sea into the store at `vivarium build` time so no per-launch warm is needed) is NOW LANDED (2026-07-24, `DECISIONS[epoch-surfaces-are-store-citizens]`): the background warmer became a store reader (`World::epoch_reduction`), so a warmed process Hits and never pours — the in-process background warming this entry shipped is now the cold-store fallback. bin/check: globe rebuilds clean (no warnings); vivarium-world lib 136/137 tests pass — the sole red (`craton_morphology::tests::the_fbm_threshold_known_bad_is_speckle_and_fails`) is the nucleation peer's uncommitted WIP, not this change (the predicted mid-flight churn; my territory does not touch the lib). Build verdict fresh — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref spikes/globe/src/main.rs (BuildReq/EpochReq, spawn_worker epoch branch, epoch_surface_tile, build_deep_time warmer, epoch_update, HUD timeline) · crates/vivarium-world/src/sea_level.rs (tectonic_surface_at_tp, derived_sea_level_at_tp — consumed, unchanged) · crates/vivarium-world/src/mantle_thermal.rs (abyssal_epochs — consumed) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/emerging_land.rs (the ASCII twin) · core/src/form-store-as-save.md (FE4) · core/src/form-core-view-wall.md (FE4) · core/src/form-builder-admission.md (FE4) · core/src/form-temporal-lod-regimes.md (regime G) · DECISIONS[craton-nucleation-and-deep-time-playback-ruled-in-scope] · DECISIONS[mantle-thermal-nomos-built-and-emergence-runs-in-time]

|decision[stage-is-a-marker-not-a-gate] :date 2026-07-24 :by joseph :status decided :topic process
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  The segment stage ladder is a **current-state marker**, not a gating/progression mechanism. Joseph: "It needs to be a 'current-state', not a gating mechanism, because new information, better organization, and ideas that are otherwise encouraged cause them to reset — which looks like a regression when it's a gate, and a progression — instead of a marker about the work that is still expected now in a segment."
  |reason  A gate reading punishes exactly the behaviors the house encourages (reorganization, strengthening attempts, fresh information — each resets stage), pressuring agents to defend rungs rather than improve segments, and makes an honest post-reorganization `draft` corpus read as "behind." The marker reading keeps stage present-tense: what checking work is expected here now. Complements FORMAT's existing "a ladder that only promotes accumulates falsehood."
  |impact  FORMAT.md stage section carries the norm; session summaries and audits report stage as work-remaining, never as altitude ("everything still at draft" is not ballast). No schema change; the rungs and down-motion rule stand.
  |ref FORMAT.md ("Stage is a current-state marker, not a gate")

|decision[craton-nucleation-growth-built-few-coherent-cratons-replace-fbm-speckle] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  BUILT (under DECISIONS[craton-nucleation-and-deep-time-playback-ruled-in-scope], the scope grant). The horizontal half of the open differentiation rate law (`#form-isostasy-column` FE(8)): craton PLACEMENT is no longer a threshold slice through a scale-free fBm field (percolation speckle — the wrong morphology class Joseph identified on the live globe, "the land rising above the water looks distinctly fBm-like"), it is a fated NUCLEATION-AND-GROWTH law. `lithosphere::{craton_sites, craton_growth, craton_weight_at_tp}`: (1) a FEW blue-noise nucleation sites (8–13, min sep 0.22 rad, fated on seed alone — a site that exists at epoch t exists at t+1); (2) each accretes a coherent craton of characteristic scale (mean radius 0.235 rad × [0.6,1.5] fated spread, smoothstep margin 0.06 rad, low-freq boundary warp so coastlines are irregular not circular); (3) growth radius scales with a cooling scalar g(T_p) — 0 at the Hadean-hot ceiling (nascent, near-water-world), exactly 1 at MANTLE_TP_C (the coherence pin — equals the live seed-only world by construction), saturating >1 into the cooling future — so cratons ACCRETE OUTWARD as the mantle cools and emergence reads as continents assembling. Sphere-continuous (all geometry from the unit vector); consumers unchanged (same column crust/keel blend, differently placed).
  |reason
    `#norm-probes-before-claims` / `#norm-regime-probes` / `#norm-probe-sensitivity`: the morphology claim ships with a probe that can FAIL, known-bad-first. MORPHOLOGY (the new conviction): `craton_morphology.rs` measures connected-component fragmentation at a MATCHED land budget (thresholding the retired fBm field to the same cratonized fraction, isolating shape from the retired field's uncalibrated area of 14–40%). Measured (L5–6, seeds 0/1/7): nucleation-growth carries a ~12% budget in 5–11 coherent cratons; the same budget of scale-free fBm shatters into 14–25 — a 2.3–2.8× separation (`the_fbm_threshold_known_bad_fragments_at_matched_area`, the known-bad the live field is measured against; `nucleation_growth_is_a_few_coherent_cratons`). Boundary coherence does NOT separate the classes (both locally compact) — reported, not the bar. PRESENT AREA still convicted by `craton_fraction_is_in_band` (seed 0 ~0.093, in the 0.072–0.204 band). MONOTONE EMERGENCE preserved (`cooling_grows_land_from_a_water_world_through_the_present`): required a SATURATING growth law — naive linear growth broke monotonicity for seed 7 (biggest cratons), because unbounded craton growth raises the global isostatic reference faster than basins deepen, re-submerging marginal land; saturating growth puts most accretion hot→present so basin-deepening then dominates. Probe: `examples/craton_morphology_probe.rs`.
  |impact  Land-fraction trajectory moved (mantle_cooling_probe, L7, post-ledger; the era-timing predicate agent queued behind this needs it): PRESENT (T_p=1550) 4.67/3.65/5.08% (was fBm 4.6/2.1/4.9% — seed 1 rose ~2.1→3.7% as nucleation gives it more coherent craton area; present-pin holds exactly = live world); COLD end (2.6 Ga, T_p=1464) 14.5/15.6/18.7% (was "11–28%", now tighter — saturating growth bounds it). NEW: cratons grow spatially over epochs (not just sea draining off static footprints). Peak stands ROSE (coherent cratons concentrate the felsic pile): live post-ledger 2192/2033/2274 m — all three now residual-amber on the soft relief clause (was seed 1/7 in-band under speckle); the rock-mass ledger still trims ~17% (felsic rebound), conservation still holds. Store INVALIDATED (the version bump + SRC_HASH move — the system working; Joseph rebuilds). Code: `lithosphere.rs` (craton_sites/craton_growth/craton_weight_at_tp + CRATON_* consts + retired fBm kept as known-bad + raw accessor; column_at_tp reads _at_tp), `craton_morphology.rs` (NEW probe module + 3 tests), `nomotheke.rs` (LITHOSPHERE version bump + prose), `sea_level.rs` (decreed-sea known-bad datum 3000→2000 m, recalibrated to the higher surface), `examples/{craton_morphology_probe(NEW), craton_frac_probe, emerged_land_probe}.rs`, `lib.rs`. `bin/check` green (137 lib tests + cli_admission + determinism clippy). Segment `#form-isostasy-column` FE(7)/FE(8)/FE(6)/FE(9)/status/Working-Notes + ASSUMPTIONS "craton geometry" — all present truth. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed. OPEN (named): differentiation DYNAMICS (sites+growth are fated geometry, not melt/plate physics); plates as placement mechanism (separate council decision stands); any claim of Earth calibration.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/lithosphere.rs (craton_sites, craton_growth, craton_weight_at_tp, craton_weight_fbm_speckle_known_bad) · crates/vivarium-world/src/craton_morphology.rs · crates/vivarium-world/src/nomotheke.rs (LITHOSPHERE) · crates/vivarium-world/src/sea_level.rs · crates/vivarium-world/examples/craton_morphology_probe.rs · core/src/form-isostasy-column.md (FE7/FE8) · ASSUMPTIONS.md ("craton geometry") · DECISIONS[craton-nucleation-and-deep-time-playback-ruled-in-scope] · DECISIONS[rock-mass-ledger-built-erosion-returns-mass-rebound-trims-the-amber]

|decision[memoized-means-store-object] :date 2026-07-24 :by joseph :status decided :topic architecture
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  "Memoized" is DEFINITIONALLY "memoized to disk" — a memo is a store object under a complete key, forever going forward, with deletion governed by supersession / Realized-vs-unrealized / regenerable-irreducible rules. Joseph: "Is there any reason ever to not just ensure that everything 'memoized' == 'memoized to disk' forever going forward? ... Please make it so, to comply with the original intent all along." Strengthens the 2026-07-09 pervasive-memoization directive from cost-conditional ("wherever recompute exceeds round-trip") to definitional, closing the loophole that phrasing left open.
  |reason
    The cost-conditional form permitted a second in-RAM memo tier for anything judged borderline — a tier where truth silently lives per-process. Paid-for same-day specimen: the mantle-thermal epoch surfaces shipped with per-process memoization only, so every fresh process re-paid every ~10 s pour; the gap was twice flagged as "owed" and sat waiting until Joseph asked why (the `#norm-declared-violation-is-not-license` pattern, caught by its author). The surviving carve-outs are not exceptions but reclassifications: (a) recompute-cheaper-than-round-trip values are not memos at all; (b) RAM caches are working-set staging of what the store owns, never a home of record. Honest cost named: write amplification under heavy iteration — a memo-GRAIN question (what deserves to be a memo boundary), not a tier question; source-hash keys make superseded generations cleanly identifiable garbage.
  |impact  `#form-store-as-save` FE(6) rewritten to the definitional form. A per-process cache holding results worth keeping past the working set is henceforth a bug against the law, not a judgment call. Eviction stays policy-only (regenerable/irreducible split, FE(7)); GC design remains open work. The in-flight epoch-store agent is building the epoch surfaces to exactly this doctrine.
  |ref core/src/form-store-as-save.md (FE 6) · core/src/form-complete-content-addressed-key.md · DECISIONS[code-to-claim-wave-council-fronts-conservation-and-execution]

|decision[epoch-surfaces-are-store-citizens] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  BUILT (Joseph's direct ask — the compliance gap minted earlier today, "why does it exist? no good reason — territory pressure," now closed). The mantle-thermal cooling chain's per-epoch global reductions — the derived sea and the rock-mass-ledger integrals (deposit thickness + post-erosion isostatic reference) — become STORE CITIZENS under complete keys, so the cost of warming an epoch belongs at `vivarium build` time and a fresh process HITS the store instead of re-paying the ~393k-cell pour every launch. Brings the tree into compliance with decided law `DECISIONS[memoized-means-store-object]` / `#form-store-as-save` FE(6) (memoized ≡ store object), not merely a recommendation.
  |reason
    GRAIN (mine to decide, chosen over-key-not-under-key): the citizen is the four f64 scalars per (seed, T_p) — pre-ledger sea, ledger deposit_m, ledger post_reference, post-erosion derived sea — NOT the big surface field. Given the scalars, every per-cell `tectonic_surface_at_tp` read is O(1), so the field regenerates at ANY resolution a viewer wants (thin-save, `#form-store-as-save` FE(7): regenerable state is not materialized; storing the L8 field would lock the sample level and over-materialize). KEYING (the one judgment call, flagged to Joseph): keyed through the declared chain HEAD `mantle-thermal` (the nomos that indexes epochs — it produces T_p) folding seed + tp_bits + the downstream chain versions (lithosphere/isostasy/hydrosphere) + `aspect=epoch-reduction`. These scalars are a memoized COMPOSITION of an already-declared chain, not a new article of law — LITHOSPHERE's own declaration already names "wiring derived-sea as its own nomos edge" a deferred owned nicety bordering the live isostasy seam — so this deliberately does NOT mint a new nomos+flux. Completeness is guaranteed by `src=SRC_HASH` on the stem (`#form-complete-content-addressed-key` FE(4): the whole-crate source digest folds every module the pour/ledger touch); the chain versions are folded for legibility. PROBES (`#norm-probes-before-claims`): (1) purity — a store Hit returns the reduction BYTE-IDENTICAL to a fresh compute, and `derived_sea_m` equals the canonical `derived_sea_level_m` (`query::tests::epoch_reduction_hit_equals_recompute_byte_identical`); (2) the payoff — `examples/epoch_store_timing.rs` run twice on one store dir (a genuine two-process measurement): cold process warms the 7-epoch ladder in ~1.9 s, warmed process in ~0.85 ms (all Hit, zero pour).
  |impact  Closes the two "owed" flags (`#form-builder-admission` Known-incomplete #5 store-side half, and the playback/first-light DECISIONS entries) — integration is replacement, both now read landed. The in-RAM caches in `sea_level` / `erosion_return` are reframed in code as WORKING-SET STAGING of the store-owned reduction (FE(6) vocabulary), primed from a store Hit via new `prime_*` fns — never a durability tier. Observe-only posture unchanged (`#form-builder-admission` FE(4)): warmer/refine threads compute off the frame path on a cold/absent store exactly as before, only faster once built. Code: `query.rs` (EpochReduction struct + `World::epoch_reduction` + key + test), `sea_level.rs` (pre-ledger cache lifted to module staging + `prime_derived_sea_pre_ledger`), `erosion_return.rs` (ledger + post-sea caches lifted to staging + `ledger_scalars`/`prime_ledger`/`prime_derived_sea_after_erosion`), `bin/vivarium.rs` (build materializes the epoch ladder), `spikes/globe/src/main.rs` (warmer + present-refine become store readers), `examples/epoch_store_timing.rs` (NEW). `bin/check` green (138 lib tests + cli_admission + determinism clippy). Segment `#form-builder-admission` FE(4)#5 → present truth. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed. OPEN (named, not this work): status-census attribution of these global-scalar roots (they land as level-less "unknown" today — honest, a global scalar has no level, but a nicer census pass could name them); wiring the derived-sea/ledger as its own flux-web nomos edge (LITHOSPHERE's still-deferred owned nicety); GC of superseded-by-SRC_HASH reduction generations (the store-wide open GC question).
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/query.rs (EpochReduction, World::epoch_reduction) · crates/vivarium-world/src/sea_level.rs (prime_derived_sea_pre_ledger) · crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion_return.rs (ledger_scalars, prime_ledger, prime_derived_sea_after_erosion) · crates/vivarium-world/src/bin/vivarium.rs (build epoch ladder) · spikes/globe/src/main.rs (warmer + refine as store readers) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/epoch_store_timing.rs · core/src/form-store-as-save.md (FE6/FE7) · core/src/form-builder-admission.md (FE4 #5) · core/src/form-complete-content-addressed-key.md (FE4) · DECISIONS[memoized-means-store-object] · DECISIONS[globe-first-light-blocks-on-isostasy-pour-never-block-violation] · DECISIONS[deep-time-playback-built-the-globe-animates-the-cooling-chain]

|decision[emerged-land-timing-clauses-convict-what-the-crude-rate-law-can-earn] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  BUILT — the emerged-land predicate's TEMPORAL clauses, `NotPredicable` while "per cycle" collapsed to per-seed with no time axis, are now split by what the DECLARED-CRUDE mantle-thermal rate law can honestly earn, and the calibration-free half convicts. `sea_level::emerged_land_timing_verdict(&[EmergedLandRecord])` (pure over a hot→cool sequence, so probe-sensitivity-testable) + `emerged_land_timing_verdict_for(seed, level)` (walks `mantle_thermal::abyssal_epochs` on the live post-ledger chain). Clauses: CONVICTED (calibration-free, depend only on the declared cooling DIRECTION + epoch ordering) — `emergence_monotone` (land fraction non-decreasing hot→cool), `starts_submerged` (hottest epoch below the band floor — the water-world start), `enters_and_stays_in_band` (reaches the early-Earth band and never overshoots the ~20% ceiling within the modeled window); NotPredicable (honestly refused) — `ga_calibrated_era`, the literature's Ga-ABSOLUTE targets ("≳10% by ~3.0 Ga"; "modern freeboard only ~2.5 Ga"), because the epoch→Ga mapping (`PRESENT_ABYSSAL_GA`, τ) is declared order-of-magnitude, not fitted, so an absolute-Ga target is a calibration the crude curve cannot earn.
  |reason
    Coordinator handed this as the natural continuation of the mantle-thermal nomos; the cooling trajectory IS the time axis those clauses always needed. Re-measured FRESH against current main (post-ledger 8d4c5cc + craton nucleation 1757c09) because two commits moved the trajectory after my earlier numbers: `mantle_cooling_probe` (level 7, seeds 0/1/7) — hot 3.6 Ga 0.00% (water-world) → present 3.2 Ga 4.67/3.65/5.08% (in-band, == live seed-only world, coherence pin) → cold 2.6 Ga 14.5/15.6/18.7%. THE FINDING MOVED, recorded per the coordinator's ask: my earlier ">20% at coldest epochs for two seeds" is GONE — nucleation's SATURATING growth bounds the cold end under the 20% ceiling, so the whole modeled Abyssal span now stays in-band (the retired dispersed-fBm world overshot to ~27% and would fail `enters_and_stays_in_band` — that clause's known-bad is a real historical shape). Honesty on the Ga clause (`#voice-discipline`, strengthen-before-soften): the crude curve happens to PASS NEAR the 10%-by-3.0-Ga literature target (10.8/11.4/13.3% at the 3.0-Ga-labeled epoch) — recorded as an encouraging coincidence, explicitly NOT convicted, because convicting it would assert a calibration I did not earn. Probes first (`#norm-probe-sensitivity`): the pure-over-slice verdict fails four synthetic temporal known-bads — a non-monotone dip (fails monotone), a >20% overshoot (fails stay-in-band), a never-emerging drowned world (fails stay-in-band), a hot-start-already-emerged world (fails submerged-start). Store law (`#form-store-as-save` FE(6), `DECISIONS[memoized-means-store-object]`): the verdict is an INSTRUMENT, not world-state — it memoizes nothing itself (recompute-is-representation), reading the store-backed epoch surfaces the epoch-store agent already built; no new per-process memo of world-state introduced.
  |impact  `sea_level.rs` (EmergedLandTimingVerdict + the two fns + `timing_verdict_convicts_the_live_cooling_chain` + `timing_verdict_catches_temporal_known_bads`; the static `emerged_land_verdict`'s per-record `timing` stays NotPredicable — a single Record has no time axis — with its doc corrected to point at the trajectory verdict); `examples/mantle_cooling_probe.rs` (prints the timing verdict). Segments to present truth: `#form-isostasy-column` FE(6) (temporal clauses split), FE(7) (the stale >20% sentence DELETED — integration-is-replacement — replaced with the in-band-stay finding + timing convictions), FE(8) (owed→split: calibration-free convicted, Ga-absolute open on a calibrated rate law), Working Notes; ordinum `promise[emerged-land]` predicate note. Still Claimed, NOT Kept. OPEN (the one blocker for the Ga clause): a CALIBRATED mantle-thermal rate law — until then "≳10% by 3.0 Ga" cannot be earned. `bin/check` green. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/sea_level.rs (emerged_land_timing_verdict) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/mantle_cooling_probe.rs · core/src/form-isostasy-column.md (FE6/7/8) · tabularium/terrestris.ordinum.udon (promise[emerged-land]) · DECISIONS[mantle-thermal-nomos-built-and-emergence-runs-in-time] · DECISIONS[craton-nucleation-growth-built-few-coherent-cratons-replace-fbm-speckle] · DECISIONS[memoized-means-store-object]

|decision[build-cli-parameterization-is-manifest-and-nomos-debt] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session 8145183f-e9f3-4836-b4c8-edc86bed663e
  OBSERVATION (Joseph flagged it: "why in the world is there a subsystem-specific flag in the global command?"). `vivarium build --epochs E` is erosion's convergence knob hoisted to the world-level CLI; water's own step count is a hardcoded `200` literal at the call site (`bin/vivarium.rs:344-345`). Both are per-nomos parameters living in the wrong place because build parameterization has no proper home yet: per-vivium "how far / at what fidelity" belongs on the MANIFEST (`#form-manifest-prescribes-vivium`, designed/partial), per-nomos convergence params belong ON the NomosDecl folded into the complete key (`#form-add-system-contract` cl.4 / `#form-complete-content-addressed-key`), and demand->materialization is the builder DAEMON's job (`#detail-builder-daemon`, unbuilt). The global command should express world-level DEMAND (target phase/region/level), not subsystem knobs. Same gap hardcodes the playback frame count (`abyssal_epochs()` = a literal 6-vec, unrelated to `--epochs` despite the shared word — a terminology collision worth removing).
  |reason  Builder v0 is a hand-cranked scaffold; the flags are expedients for the manifest+demand layer the segments design but that isn't built. NEAR-MISS recorded (do not re-walk): "one --epochs driving slow erosion + fast water" LOOKS like a multirate violation (`#form-scale-separation-directional`) but is NOT — water's param is `erosion_epochs` (it names which eroded bed to settle onto, dependency-by-key `#form-depend-by-key-never-latest`; its own steps are the separate hardcoded 200). The coupling is correct; the defect is layering, and the parameter name falsifies the plausible multirate story on inspection.
  |impact  Full findings + un-conflated work items in `msc/build-parameterization-findings-2026-07-24.md` (frame-density densification with nested-sampling care; move convergence params to NomosDecl+key; disambiguate "epochs"; water's 200 literal moves with them). Harness-upgrade residue — instrument, not claim canon; no segment changed. When a harness owner lands parameter-home decisions, they may warrant their own DECISIONS row + a build/manifest ops segment.
  |ref msc/build-parameterization-findings-2026-07-24.md · core/src/form-manifest-prescribes-vivium.md · core/src/form-add-system-contract.md · core/src/detail-builder-daemon.md · crates/vivarium-world/src/bin/vivarium.rs

|decision[cooling-stages-take-the-word-back-and-densify-by-bisection] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session e94cd6cf-a5cd-41c5-afe3-80c48220bbf7
  BUILT — items 1 and 3 of `DECISIONS[build-cli-parameterization-is-manifest-and-nomos-debt]`'s findings note, which had sat unworked (Joseph: "whole last session was ostensibly predicated on it, but i haven't heard anything about it since"). VOCABULARY: `LEXICON`'s `term[epoch]` already reserves the word EXCLUSIVELY for the erosion solver's step unit, so the collision had a decided answer nobody had looked up — the CLI's `--epochs` is correctly named and `mantle_thermal::abyssal_epochs()` was the violator. Renamed `cooling_stages()`, taking the noun from `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` FE(8-9), which already calls that chain a STAGE chain. No term coined. DENSITY: `cooling_stages_refined(k)` bisects to 5*2^k+1 stages, and `--frames N` on `vivarium build` rounds UP to the next nested count and says so in the log.
  |reason
    THE ONE JUDGMENT CALL, and it decides the shape: exact nesting vs the requested number. Each stage's T_p is folded into its store key, so a "denser" chain whose shared stages differ by one ULP does not refine anything — it orphans every memo on the chain and silently recomputes the world. Interpolating to exactly 60 frames, or refusing the request, each cost that subset property; rounding up keeps it, and the property is worth more than the round number. Built on MICRO-GA INTEGERS rather than floats so the nesting is bit-exact by construction rather than by floating-point argument (`#form-complete-content-addressed-key`; the power-of-two subset argument is Gear-Wells', `#detail-seam-precedents` FE(5)). PROBES that can fail (`#norm-probes-before-claims`): a MIGRATION GUARD pinning the canonical six to the authored f64 literals' exact bits (every existing store is keyed on them — a rename that moved them would read as a performance regression, not as the key change it is), and a per-level bisection-survival test. MEASURED, not argued: against a store built at 81 stages, `--frames 6 / 11 / 21` are 6 / 11 / 21 store HITS, 0 computed, in microseconds.
  |impact  `mantle_thermal.rs` (cooling_stages, cooling_stages_refined, stage_count, refinements_for, 4 new tests); mechanical rename at 6 call sites incl. `spikes/globe/src/main.rs`; `bin/vivarium.rs` (`--frames`). Segment `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` to present truth: new FE(8) owns densification-by-bisection with the measurement; FE(9)/FE(10) renumbered; the Working Note "densification must nest" retired into FE(8) as built, and the "two epochs" note replaced by the LEXICON resolution. OPEN, un-conflated and NOT done here: findings items 2 and 4 — erosion iterations and water's hardcoded 200 steps still live on the global command instead of the NomosDecl folded into their keys, with the manifest owning per-vivium fidelity. `bin/check` green (153 lib + 3 CLI). Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/mantle_thermal.rs (cooling_stages_refined) · crates/vivarium-world/src/bin/vivarium.rs (--frames) · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md (FE8) · LEXICON.udon (term[epoch]) · msc/build-parameterization-findings-2026-07-24.md · DECISIONS[build-cli-parameterization-is-manifest-and-nomos-debt]

|decision[the-build-reader-is-one-poller-and-it-counts-what-it-cannot-show] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session e94cd6cf-a5cd-41c5-afe3-80c48220bbf7
  BUILT (Joseph's ask: "I mostly want to be able to run the world building and see it happening or watch what happened"). `vivarium watch` — the globe repainted as store roots land. Live follows a running builder; `--replay` walks the store's landing history. They are ONE code path below the frame source, honouring `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` FE(5) as a design constraint rather than restating it ("built as two features they become two, and they will disagree"). The reader takes no lock and writes nothing, so an explorer cannot disturb a builder (`#form-builder-admission`).
  |reason
    WHAT THE INSTRUMENT MEASURES is the part that convicts, and it was not the goal going in. FE(2) claims a build without a time-index in its keys is "opaque between started and finished" — a checkable statement about this tree, so `watch::interior` counts distinct time-index values per nomos instead of restating it. On a completed L7 build: mantle-thermal 81 (tp_bits); erosion-tile 1; water-tile 1 — "endpoint only, no interior." The one chain the segment calls a stage chain is the only one that has one. HONESTY ON THE ORDERING (`#voice-discipline`): root files carry no world-time, so replay orders by root LANDING time — build history, not world-time — and the command says so on screen every run rather than letting the animation imply otherwise. The interior census is exactly the measurement of that gap. TWO WRONG READINGS, both pinned by tests because both flatter the tree: water's time-index is `steps`, NOT the `eepochs` selecting which eroded bed it settles onto (the near-miss already recorded in `DECISIONS[build-cli-parameterization-is-manifest-and-nomos-debt]`); and the cooling reductions key under `mantle-thermal` with `aspect=epoch-reduction`, so looking for a nomos named "epoch-reduction" reports no interior for the only object that has one — I made that error first and kept it in a comment.
  |impact  `watch.rs` (NEW: landings, frame_bounds, interior, honesty_block, 5 tests); `bin/vivarium.rs` (`cmd_watch`). Also surfaces epistemic state the registry has always DECLARED and that only ever reached `vivarium status` — a command nobody has open while watching a build: physics tier declared/derived, and the provisional flag. Segment `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` to present truth: FE(2) gains the measured interior census, FE(5) records the poller as built with its honest ordering, Epistemic Status moves the poller and the census from Unbuilt to Built, and the stale Working Note naming WATER as the cheapest first epsilon target is corrected — `#obs-water-fill-never-settles` blocked that, and erosion is the unblocked candidate. OUTLINE §III's "operated instrument" gap NARROWED, not closed (watchpoints and steerable time regimes still homeless). `bin/check` green (153 lib + 3 CLI). Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/watch.rs · crates/vivarium-world/src/bin/vivarium.rs (cmd_watch) · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md (FE2/FE5) · core/OUTLINE.md · core/src/obs-water-fill-never-settles.md · DECISIONS[cooling-stages-take-the-word-back-and-densify-by-bisection]

|decision[erosion-convergence-gate-is-a-measured-no-go-and-the-count-must-not-be-relocated] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session e94cd6cf-a5cd-41c5-afe3-80c48220bbf7
  NO-GO, measured. Item 2 of `DECISIONS[build-cli-parameterization-is-manifest-and-nomos-debt]` (move per-nomos convergence params off the global CLI onto the NomosDecl, folded into the key) was about to be built as written when Joseph flagged the gap-row phrasing: "that's something that sounds like exactly opposite of the vision of this program." He was right, and the sharper reading is the load-bearing one -- RELOCATING AN ARBITRARY INTEGER FROM A CLI FLAG INTO THE DECLARATION LAYER MAKES IT LOOK PRINCIPLED WITHOUT MAKING IT CONVICTABLE, which is strictly worse than leaving it where it visibly is an expedient. `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` FE(4) already says the right thing: the CRITERION is what gets declared and keyed, and the count becomes an output.
  |reason
    So the question became whether erosion can HAVE a criterion, and that is measurable rather than arguable -- the discipline `#obs-water-fill-never-settles` paid for the day before (build the gate, and building it forces you to ask what the thing converges TO). MEASURED (`examples/erosion_settle_probe`, L9, 64x64, four footprints, two configurations each, deterministic): (1) driven -- in the builder's own kernel setup -- the per-epoch residual is CONSTANT to four significant figures for 400 epochs on three of four footprints, and equals the mean uplift rate (0.470 vs 0.501, 0.470 vs 0.500, 0.469 vs 0.500); (2) undriven -- uplift zeroed -- those same three report EXACTLY ZERO at every epoch, while the fourth falls 5.5e-2 -> 8.7e-4. The pair is the discriminator; either configuration alone cannot tell "eroding in exact balance with uplift" from "not eroding at all". A CORRECTION I MADE MID-PROBE AND AM RECORDING BECAUSE THE REASONING IS THE USEFUL PART: my first version built Fluvial bare, without the uplift and precip-weight fields `query::World::erosion_tile` supplies, and so measured a kernel nobody runs -- it showed a slowly-falling residual and would have warranted a tolerance. WHY THE THREE ARE INERT: they hold 0, 5 and 5 subaerial cells of 4096, against 3911 on the one that erodes. Fluvial incision is subaerial, so this is the kernel behaving CORRECTLY -- the consequence is about SCHEDULING, not the kernel: at an Abyssal land fraction a whole-world sweep spends its authored epochs mostly where every epoch is a no-op plus one uplift addition. CONSEQUENCE for the gate: sustained uplift leaves no zero-residual equilibrium, so a near-stationarity tolerance either never fires or fires immediately on a tile that is merely INERT -- and the second is the dangerous failure, since it certifies convergence exactly where nothing was computed.
  |impact  NEW segment `#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound` (empirical, both halves independently falsifiable) + `examples/erosion_settle_probe` (NEW) + OUTLINE row. `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` to present truth: FE(4) keeps its claim but no longer presumes the criterion is a residual TOLERANCE, and now carries the do-not-relocate-the-number warning; the Working Note naming erosion as the unblocked epsilon candidate -- written by me two commits earlier, this session -- is CORRECTED, since the measurement refutes it. `ASSUMPTIONS.md` "erosion run length" row rewritten to what was measured (wrong in BOTH directions: over-run on the inert majority, under-run ~36x on the working minority). NOT DONE, and named rather than attempted: the erosion-uplift BALANCE criterion the working class actually needs (open research), and the no-subaerial-cells PRECONDITION for the inert class -- which is the one honest criterion available today, is convictable, and belongs with `#form-builder-admission` as a scheduling claim rather than in the kernel. NOT MEASURED: the build-time cost of inert epochs, in seconds -- the waste is demonstrated in kernel work only. `bin/check` green. Build verdict FRESH -- proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref core/src/obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound.md · crates/vivarium-world/examples/erosion_settle_probe.rs · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md (FE4) · ASSUMPTIONS.md · core/src/obs-water-fill-never-settles.md · DECISIONS[build-cli-parameterization-is-manifest-and-nomos-debt]

|decision[the-manifest-prescribes-and-the-flags-only-override] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session e94cd6cf-a5cd-41c5-afe3-80c48220bbf7
  BUILT — the actual complaint behind `DECISIONS[build-cli-parameterization-is-manifest-and-nomos-debt]`, which I had first read too narrowly. Joseph: "I mostly was complaining about cli overriding the world manifest etc." The manifest carried identity and label only, so every build was hand-cranked with flags and a vivium did not remember what it was meant to be. `spec.rs` now implements the DEMAND bucket `#form-manifest-prescribes-vivium` FE(2) has specified since 2026-07-12 — order, target_phase, level, frames, erosion_epochs, water_steps — and CLI flags became a one-run OVERRIDE of it, with every override named in the build log. Also: every command now ANNOUNCES which world directory it resolved and WHY (explicit arg / $VIVARIUM_WORLD / shared default), because the dir is optional everywhere and two silent fallbacks meant the commonest invocation acted on a world the caller never named ("I have no idea what world manifest they're running against"). And `bin/vivarium` wraps the cargo incantation.
  |reason
    THE JUDGMENT CALL, and it is the one the prior no-go constrains: the two arbitrary counts (erosion epochs, water steps) go in the MANIFEST and NOT on `NomosDecl`. The distinction is the whole point and it is a truth condition, not a preference — the manifest records WHAT THIS WORLD ASKED FOR, which is true of an arbitrary number; a declaration field would assert it as part of the declared law, which is false (`#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound`). Demand is NOT keyed: it changes what gets built and in what order, never what a built artifact contains (`#form-depend-by-key-never-latest`; LEXICON `beacon`). CONVICTED INCIDENTALLY, and the nicest evidence in the change: moving level and erosion_epochs out of flags and into the manifest, then rebuilding with no flags at all, produced 0 COMPUTED / ALL HITS — same keys, different home for the request. BACKWARD COMPATIBILITY as a truth condition too: demand keys are optional-and-defaulting while identity keys stay required, so a manifest written before the bucket existed opens as THE WORLD IT ALWAYS WAS rather than erroring or opening as a different world; pinned by test. Water's hardcoded `200` at the CLI call site (findings item 4) moved with them.
  |impact  `spec.rs` (Demand struct, serialize/parse, 4 new tests incl. the pre-demand-manifest guard and the demand-is-not-identity guard); `bin/vivarium.rs` (manifest-first demand, override logging, announce_world on all six commands, status prints demand, module header rewritten); `bin/vivarium` (NEW wrapper); `README.md` run section rewritten. Segment `#form-manifest-prescribes-vivium` to present truth: NEW FE(5) owns demand-is-not-identity with its conviction and the bounds on what may move here; FE(5) "not fully built" becomes FE(6) naming precisely what is and is not implemented; Epistemic Status upgrades the not-keyed property to `exact`. STILL UNBUILT and named: permits, beacons, watchpoints — the demand-POSTURE half of FE(2) — and law/generator version pinning at Realization. `bin/check` green (156 lib + 3 CLI). Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/spec.rs (Demand) · crates/vivarium-world/src/bin/vivarium.rs · bin/vivarium · core/src/form-manifest-prescribes-vivium.md (FE5/FE6) · README.md · DECISIONS[build-cli-parameterization-is-manifest-and-nomos-debt] · DECISIONS[erosion-convergence-gate-is-a-measured-no-go-and-the-count-must-not-be-relocated]

|decision[no-depiction-without-a-world-referent] :date 2026-07-24 :by joseph :status decided :topic architecture
  :session e94cd6cf-a5cd-41c5-afe3-80c48220bbf7
  NORM, Joseph's articulation, offered as wording to adopt: "visual affordances that don't have a world reality are only acceptable with strong caveats temporarily for illuminating other real phenomena" -- "while strongly discouraging it." Segment home: `#norm-no-depiction-without-referent`. It is adjacent to but NOT covered by `#form-core-view-wall`, which owns AUTHORITY (who may author world state); this owns DEPICTION (what may be shown). A view can satisfy that wall completely -- observe-only, no reverse dependency, no evolution knobs -- and still interpolate frames or smooth a seam, which manufactures world content in the one channel nobody audits.
  |reason
    WHY THE BAR IS HIGHER THAN ORDINARY HONESTY, and this is the reason the norm is worth its own segment: the viewer's trained eye is a primary INSTRUMENT here, and one whose reach differs in kind from a probe's. Joseph, same day: "my brain is very highly tuned to be able to notice if there's something in the visual evolution that does not seem 'natural' (especially when we get to later erosion etc.). And even when this was just play, it was clear that it was one of the fastest ways to reveal missing physics -- when something is acting contrary to what my mind has hundreds of thousands of hours of remembered patterns of..." A probe convicts one declared thing someone already thought to ask about (`#norm-probes-before-claims`); the eye fires on violations NOBODY HAS DECLARED YET. So an unreal affordance does not merely mislead once -- it DECALIBRATES the instrument, producing false detections and destroying trust in true ones. The cost is an entire epistemic channel, not a wrong frame. MY ONE ADDITION, flagged as a reading rather than Joseph's words (segment FE(4)): for a VISUAL instrument a text-only caveat is not a strong caveat, because a viewer engaged in pattern-matching is not reading the HUD -- that is the faculty in use. "Strong" therefore has to mean structurally self-announcing: relief exaggeration x20 passes (unmistakable to the eye; the label is a reminder), interpolated intermediate states fail (designed to look like continuous physics, so no label stops the eye reading them as motion). DISTILLING, NOT INVENTING: the tree already practised this without a citable home -- the globe's honesty line refuses "cosmetic interpolation" between epochs, `#form-sphere-continuous-surface-fields` records that the cube-seam fix had to be a domain change "not edge blending or cosmetic clamps", `#detail-drainage-dependency-planning` FE(4) refuses cosmetic clamps on floating mesas.
  |impact  NEW segment `#norm-no-depiction-without-referent` + OUTLINE row (I, beside the view wall). LIVE CONSEQUENCE, already relayed to the explorer agent: `erosion-tile` has exactly ONE distinct time-index (`watch::interior`), so animating erosion by interpolating between endpoints would synthesise precisely the smooth evolution Joseph watches FOR -- a renderer's guess wearing the appearance of a result, and the worst available failure for this instrument. Honest depiction is a discontinuity plus a statement that there is nothing between; the repair belongs to the BUILDER (materialise intermediate stages), never the view. That makes Joseph's exploration need a forcing function on the stage-chain work rather than a separate track. ACCEPTED COST, stated in the segment: honest depiction of an incomplete world is LESS legible than a smoothed one -- unbuilt looks unbuilt, a system with no interior jumps. OPEN: no instrument enumerates the live views' unreal affordances (relief exaggeration and build-state colour are the two known, both declared); and the three-condition exception has no worked example yet -- the first should be recorded, since an exception never exercised drifts into an absolute and an absolute gets quietly broken instead of openly invoked.
  |ref core/src/norm-no-depiction-without-referent.md · core/src/form-core-view-wall.md · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md (FE2) · core/src/form-sphere-continuous-surface-fields.md · core/src/detail-drainage-dependency-planning.md · crates/vivarium-world/src/watch.rs (interior)

|decision[the-explorer-is-an-instrument-and-the-wall-is-a-handle] :date 2026-07-24 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session e94cd6cf-a5cd-41c5-afe3-80c48220bbf7
  BUILT (Joseph's directive: "lose the spikes and old stuff -- put it in .archive and launch an agent to orchestrate building a principled globe renderer that is the 3d version of vivarium watch", and the purpose clause that settled the design: "my exploration needs supporting so we can actually verify the rationality of the systems as they get developed"). `crates/vivarium-explore` replaces `spikes/{globe,worldview}` (retired to `archive/`), leaving ONE view crate. Reached as `vivarium explore`, dispatched by exec to a separate binary -- a real in-process subcommand would link Bevy into `vivarium status` and break the dependency-direction wall the workspace manifest states as a rule.
  |reason
    THE WALL BECAME A MECHANISM, which is the load-bearing change and not a convenience: `Store::open_read_only` serves reads and refuses puts with `PermissionDenied`, COUNTING refusals, so a view cannot author a citizen regardless of who wrote it -- and the explorer displays its own refused-write count, making the wall a number rather than a promise. `status`/`info`/`watch` share the handle, which closed a LATENT violation nobody had named: `globe::render` reached `erosion_tile`, which computes and writes on a miss. This is the mechanization `#form-store-as-save` FE(8) had carried as compliance debt since it was written. THE INSTRUMENT CAUGHT THREE DEFECTS IN ITSELF, which is the class it exists for: (1) it called `World::water_tile`, which runs the fill kernel on a miss -- 44 s to first frame, cold-re-settling the planet to draw it; (2) the census laddered roots from other source trees, so provenance read "watered" while the surface read "100% prior", two adjacent lines contradicting each other; (3) that contradiction led to `#obs-coarse-view-draws-the-uncarved-prior` -- an `ErodedRegion` answers only at its own level or finer, so any view coarser than the build level silently draws the uncarved prior over a store full of carved tiles, and nothing looks wrong. SMOOTHNESS WITHOUT TWEENING (`#norm-no-depiction-without-referent`): the view samples 120 cooling stages by the same nested bisection the builder uses, so its ladder is a strict superset containing every built stage bit-exactly -- 81 of 161 are store citizens on Joseph's world, and the timeline paints which are which. Unbuilt stages are real law evaluations, labelled, written nowhere.
  |impact  NEW `crates/vivarium-explore`; `spikes/{globe,worldview}` -> `archive/{globe-spike,worldview-spike}`; `Store::open_read_only` + refused-write counter + 2 tests; paint modes (surface / provenance / water / seam) -- the `water-tile` field is drawn for the FIRST time by any view; sighting capture (`C`) writing full state + screenshot beside the world, never in the store; a NOT MODELLED block derived from live law and census. Segments to present truth: `#form-core-view-wall` gains FE(5) (observation density is not authorship) and FE(6) (the wall is a handle), and its Epistemic Status debt list is REPLACED -- items 1-2 described `spikes/worldview` and its `VIVARIUM_ALLOW_VIEW_EVOLUTION` waiver, both of which no longer exist, so leaving them would have been a segment asserting stale debt as current; `#form-store-as-save` FE(8) splits mechanized-view-half from remaining-builder-half and gains FE(9) (the roots census is a build-state ladder; build-state is a fact about the SAVE, not about geology); NEW `#obs-coarse-view-draws-the-uncarved-prior` + convicting unit test. OPEN, named not attempted: erosion's missing time-interior is BUILDER work and was deliberately not touched (`watch::interior` = 1); the ASCII `globe::render` is unaudited against the depiction norm; the source-hash digest covers `src/bin/`, so editing CLI HELP TEXT invalidates the whole store -- excluding `src/bin/*.rs` cannot under-key (separate crates the lib cannot reference, compiler-checkable) but that is world-frame keying and is Joseph's call; and at L9 the water nomos produces ZERO inland standing water (1,376,245 wet cells, none above the datum) -- surfaced by the instrument, wants a probe before it is a claim. Build verdict FRESH -- proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-explore · crates/vivarium-world/src/store.rs (open_read_only) · crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (the convicting test) · core/src/form-core-view-wall.md (FE5/FE6) · core/src/form-store-as-save.md (FE8/FE9) · core/src/obs-coarse-view-draws-the-uncarved-prior.md · core/src/norm-no-depiction-without-referent.md · DECISIONS[no-depiction-without-a-world-referent] · DECISIONS[core-view-wall-observe-only]

|decision[erosion-settle-history-is-a-materialized-stage-chain] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT — the erosion time-interior, CONSOLIDATION-STATUS open-work item 1 and the binding constraint on the eye instrument (`DECISIONS[no-depiction-without-a-world-referent]` made the view side illegal, so the repair had to be the builder's). `World::erosion_tile_staged` walks a fixed ladder `stride, 2·stride, …, epochs`, seeds stage k from stage k−s's stored heights, and lands every rung as a store citizen under the EXISTING key shape (`epochs` was already in the key; what was missing was that anything interior ever landed). Stage density is the manifest demand field `erosion_stage_stride` (default 5; `vivarium demand erosion_stage_stride=N`; deliberately NO new build flag — the CLI-cleanup line, Joseph mid-session: most of these "shouldn't have been flags in the first place"). Each computed stage records its final-epoch mean |Δh| as an `aspect=stage-residual` sibling (one f32, reader `erosion_stage_residual`) — the tree's first recorded ε, and it is measured DATA, not a criterion: `#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound` stands untouched.
  |reason
    THE JUDGMENT CALL is stride-in-demand-not-key, and it rests on a conviction rather than a preference: a stage at epochs=k is BIT-IDENTICAL whether built as a chain rung or a one-shot run (the per-epoch step is pure in the height field plus keyed inputs — `Fluvial` carries no cross-epoch state beyond `h`), so the stride provably never touches artifact content. `query::tests::staged_chain_is_bit_identical_to_one_shot` is both the proof and the tripwire — its failure is the signal the stride has become identity and must move into the key. Consequences bought by that property: every EXISTING store stays valid (the endpoint key is unchanged — `first-light` kept its identity); an endpoint-only world gains its interior for the cost of one settle history (convicted: `endpoint_only_world_gains_its_interior_and_readers_see_one_surface`); and the ladder is a function of `(epochs, stride)`, never of store contents (`#form-depend-by-key-never-latest` — no "largest built stage" scan). READERS made stage-safe: `load_eroded_regions_where` dedupes to ONE region per tile — the latest accepted stage — because a surface is one moment, not a blend of moments (same fault class as the stale-src ribbon); replay composes correctly through the same dedupe ("landed by frame n" → the latest stage as of that frame); watch coverage takes max-epochs; census and coverage skip `aspect=` metadata roots. The ε home (sibling artifact over widening the memo) keeps pre-chain readers decoding stage bytes as a bare height field; provisional until a second kernel records one. Incidental fix: a HashMap in the first draft of the dedupe was rejected by the repo's own determinism clippy — the discipline works — replaced with BTreeMap; and the segment's Epistemic Status cited densification as FE(8), stale since the 2026-07-27 renumber, corrected to FE(9).
  |impact  MEASURED on `first-light` (L9, 384 tiles, stride 5): erosion sweep with full staging 12.4 s, 8 stages + 8 residuals per tile; `watch::interior` now reports `erosion-tile` 8 fresh distinct epochs (9 across all build history — the ninth is a stale-src cohort at epochs=60), vs 1 before; water-tile is now the only relaxation kernel with no interior. Files: `query.rs` (erosion_tile_staged, erosion_stage_residual + key, dedupe, 2 new tests), `erosion.rs` (with_heights pub(crate)), `spec.rs` (demand field + test), `bin/vivarium.rs` (staged sweep, demand field, usage), `watch.rs` (coverage max + aspect skip). Segments to present truth: `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` (FE2 census, FE3 first ε, FE8 first materialized-only instance, FE10, Built/Unbuilt, ε-home working note resolved provisionally), `#detail-erosion-composition` (new FE2, renumber), `#form-manifest-prescribes-vivium` (FE5 membership test, FE6 field list), OUTLINE (row parentheticals; §III gap row updated — the params-on-global-command clause was stale since the manifest decision, replaced by the NEW open item this work exposes: epoch-ordered erosion playback, the first honest world-time scrub, unblocked and unbuilt), ASSUMPTIONS (stride row). OPEN, named not attempted: epoch-ordered playback; stage-major build scheduling as its builder-side alternative; the no-subaerial-cells precondition (unchanged); and — from the LEM-literature sidebar (msc/research-lem-sota/, still folding in sub-readings at commit time) — the INTERIOR-ACCURACY question, now stated in the segment as an honesty clause: Braun–Willett's accuracy claim excepts the transient at large timestep, so the chain makes the settle history addressable and watchable WITHOUT certifying intermediate accuracy (candidate cures in the sidebar: a-priori response time T_A=β/K at n=1, χ-shape criterion, our epoch's Courant number — each convictable, none built). `bin/check` green (164 lib + 3 CLI, measured). Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/query.rs (erosion_tile_staged) · crates/vivarium-world/src/spec.rs (erosion_stage_stride) · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md · core/src/detail-erosion-composition.md (FE2) · core/src/form-manifest-prescribes-vivium.md (FE5) · ASSUMPTIONS.md (erosion stage stride) · CONSOLIDATION-STATUS.md (open-work 1) · DECISIONS[no-depiction-without-a-world-referent] · DECISIONS[the-manifest-prescribes-and-the-flags-only-override] · DECISIONS[erosion-convergence-gate-is-a-measured-no-go-and-the-count-must-not-be-relocated]

|decision[the-explorer-scrubs-world-time-and-the-cohort-is-half-the-key] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT — the epoch-ordered erosion scrub, named as open work by `DECISIONS[erosion-settle-history-is-a-materialized-stage-chain]` the same day and unblocked by it. `vivarium explore` gains a fourth lens (`E`): every tile on screen at the SAME epoch, stepped or played across the 8 materialized stages. It is not new machinery — it is `load_eroded_regions_where` under a different key predicate, which is what FE(5)'s one-mechanism claim predicted. Joseph's prompting question was the whole brief: watching `--replay` he saw only build-state colours flip — "I don't see any difference from the beginning of replay to the end... Were you expecting any kind of visible erosion?" He was right twice over, and the second way is the finding below.
  |reason
    THE PREDICATE NEEDS TWO FIELDS, NOT ONE, and this is the claim worth keeping (segment `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` FE(11), new). Holding `epochs` fixed gives one world-moment; holding it fixed ACROSS SOURCE COHORTS gives a picture of two different kernels ordered as though the difference were elapsed world-time. That is the stale-`src` two-datum fault moved onto the time axis, and it is strictly worse there: a mixed SPACE datum shows a coverage ribbon a viewer can see, a mixed TIME datum shows smooth plausible change — exactly the decalibration `#norm-no-depiction-without-referent` FE(2) exists to prevent. So the reader groups by `(src, epochs)`, offers the cohort with a real interior, and names it on every frame. `watch::erosion_cohorts` + 3 tests (never-merge across trees; endpoint-only cohorts sort behind real chains; raggedness reported rather than smoothed). A cohort that is NOT the running binary's is still shown — faithfully, under its own name, with the digest on screen — because refusing is not more honest than naming, and on a tree whose source moves hourly a refusing instrument shows nothing at all.
    NO DENSITY DIAL, deliberately, and the absence is the law: erosion is materialized-only (FE(8)), so `--frames` reaches the cooling chain and nothing reaches this one. Eight stages over a planet IS a slideshow, and the pull toward tweening is STRONGER after the builder made the sequence nearly continuous than it was when there was only an endpoint. Refused; the view instead states, on each stage, which epochs it sits between and that nothing exists there.
    THE MEASUREMENT THAT FORCED THE DISPLAY DESIGN, taken off the store before any pixel was drawn: mean |Δh| against the uncarved prior runs 3.83 m at epoch 5 to 25.56 m at epoch 40, monotone — against relief of KILOMETRES. In hypsometric colour forty epochs of erosion are invisible, which is the honest answer to Joseph's question and not a rendering failure. Hence paint `5` (change): signed Δ vs the uncarved initial topography, a real quantity of two stored fields. SIGNED because the same measurement showed 88–91% of cells RISE and only 2.7–5.6% FALL — the kernel's uplift driver acts nearly everywhere while fluvial incision is a thin minority (`#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound`, seen from the view side). A one-sided "erosion" ramp would have painted uplift and called it carving. Ramp scale is FIXED and viewer-cycled, never auto-fitted per frame: an auto-fit renormalizes each step of a scrub whose entire subject is the growth, hiding it inside the display of it.
  |impact  `crates/vivarium-explore`: `Lens::Erosion`, `lens::Chain` + `read_residuals` (residuals read BY COHORT — `World::erosion_stage_residual` builds its key with the running binary's digest and structurally cannot see a previous world's history), `Paint::Change` + `CellFacts.change_m`, `FaceInput.change`, chain timeline + residual line + cohort-provenance line on the HUD, `VIVARIUM_EROSION=<i>` autoshot idiom, sightings record `epochs=`/`src=` so a hunch on the settle history names a KEY rather than a description. `crates/vivarium-world/src/watch.rs`: `erosion_cohorts` + `ErosionCohort`, `key_field` made pub (a second hand-rolled key parser is a parser that can disagree). Segments to present truth: `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` NEW FE(11) (world-time reading + the cohort constraint), out-of-bounds renumbered to FE(12) and widened, Built/Unbuilt corrected — the explorer half is built, `vivarium watch --replay` is the remaining half; `#norm-no-depiction-without-referent` FE(5) rewritten to PRESENT truth (its concrete case, `erosion-tile` with one time-index, no longer exists) and gains the after-the-repair reading plus the law-evaluable/materialized-only permission distinction; OUTLINE §III gap row narrowed.
    FINDING SURFACED BY THE INSTRUMENT, offered not claimed: in the change paint the SEAFLOOR rises across the whole settle history — the ocean shell goes from near-zero at epoch 5 to broadly positive at epoch 40 — so the uplift nomos appears to act on submarine cells at the same order as subaerial ones (sampled uplift tile: 0.18–0.85 m/epoch, no subaerial gate visible from the view side). Whether that is intended is a physics question this crate cannot adjudicate; it wants a probe. It is exactly the class Joseph's eye was meant to catch, caught by giving the eye something to look at.
    NOT DONE, named: `vivarium watch --replay` still orders by landing time (same predicate, a census it already parses); no stage-to-stage RATE channel (only cumulative-vs-prior); render is still whole-globe L5–L9, so a 20 km cell is the finest geomorphology anyone can see and the 866 m maximum incision is one cell — the per-region quadtree is the next thing and is untouched here. `bin/check` green. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-explore/src/lens.rs (Chain) · crates/vivarium-explore/src/paint.rs (Change) · crates/vivarium-world/src/watch.rs (erosion_cohorts) · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md (FE11) · core/src/norm-no-depiction-without-referent.md (FE5) · core/OUTLINE.md · DECISIONS[erosion-settle-history-is-a-materialized-stage-chain] · DECISIONS[no-depiction-without-a-world-referent] · DECISIONS[the-explorer-is-an-instrument-and-the-wall-is-a-handle]

|decision[the-epoch-count-has-a-derivation-and-the-first-beacon-aims-at-it] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT, under Joseph's grant ("bring it closer and closer... more physical, more correct, more realistic. Find the things that move it forward with the highest leverage. Not shortcuts to make me happy — real work toward the goal"). Two pieces, physics first. (1) `Fluvial::response_census` — per-tile Courant number of the erosional kinematic wave and the DISCRETE A-PRIORI RESPONSE TIME (sum of d/(k_dt·A^m) down each flow path; at n=1 celerity is slope-independent, Whipple & Tucker 1999, so this is computable from the network before running — the literature sidebar's central recommendation, replacing the convergence gate that is a measured no-go). (2) The FIRST LIVE BEACON (LEXICON `beacon`, exactly as the settled term specifies: a standing spec-persisted (region, level) priority in the manifest demand bucket): `spec::Beacon`, one region at its own level with its own response-time-derived erosion demand, swept by `vivarium build` after the whole-world floor. Erosion-only (fine-level water blocked on the step-size question, `#obs-water-fill-never-settles`); single-beacon; no per-beacon policy yet.
  |reason
    MEASURED, and the numbers decided everything. L9 landiest tiles (live world, network census of the eroded bed): Courant p50 ≈ 0.02, max ≈ 0.11 — the epoch is an order of magnitude INSIDE Braun–Willett's Appendix B accuracy bound (the sidebar settled that bound in closed form: transient VALIDATED below Δt < C_max·10^4/n_x, not excluded — my committed honesty clause was slightly too strong and is corrected in the segment), so the stage chain's interior states are honestly resolved at L9. Channel response ≈ 20–70 epochs, so the authored 40 is ~one channel-crossing and the still-falling epoch-400 residual is the HILLSLOPE clock — a reframe of `#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound` FE(5), recorded there. Candidate L13 patches inside the landiest L9 tile (f1 (0,320)): 94% subaerial, ~1000 channel cells, Courant max ≈ 1.0 (the boundary, exactly as ×16-per-level scaling predicts), response ≈ 300 epochs. THE BEACON AIMS AT THE MEASUREMENT: f1 L13 (640,5376) 4×4 tiles, epochs=300 (one full response time — a derived count, the first non-arbitrary epoch number in the tree), stride=10 (30 scrubbable stages/tile). Built into first-light in 4.6 s; `watch::interior` erosion-tile distinct went 8 → 34 fresh, matching the predicted union (8 global ∪ 30 beacon − 4 overlap) — a checked prediction, not a coincidence. CARVING, measured (`examples/patch_carve_probe`): |h(300)−h(10)| mean 186 m, max 1200 m over 65 536 cells — the first dissection at a scale where geomorphology is visible to the eye. MID-PROBE CORRECTIONS recorded because the reasoning is the useful part: the settle probe's 3911-subaerial footprint is land UPLIFT RAISED during its 400-epoch run, not initial land (first draft measured 165 and exposed the confound); and a first-draft HashMap in the region dedupe was rejected by the determinism clippy — BTreeMap landed.
  |impact  `erosion.rs` (ResponseCensus + response_census; heights saved/restored — the census must not advance the world); `spec.rs` (Beacon, parse/serialize, all-fields-required + roundtrip + pre-beacon-manifest tests); `bin/vivarium.rs` (beacon sweep with the erosion admission gate, demand field incl. `beacon=none`, usage); examples `response_time_probe` (live-world census, stale-src fallback stated on screen) + `patch_carve_probe`. Segments to present truth: `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` FE(8) (three clauses: total-order purity — claim the CONVICTION, not the purity, per the sidebar's field survey; the CONVERSE INVARIANT — cadence may partition the epoch sequence, never alter Δt/count/uplift placement, the field's measured wild-failure mode; transient-accuracy-below-a-stated-bound with the measured Courant numbers); `#form-manifest-prescribes-vivium` FE(6) (first beacon built; permits/watchpoints/policy still not); `#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound` Working Notes (the count question reframed a-priori; χ-shape test named as the candidate criterion, unbuilt); ASSUMPTIONS 'erosion run length' retire-by column. Coordination: `msc/agent-briefs/l13-patch-coords.md` for the explorer agent (working `crates/vivarium-explore` + `watch.rs` concurrently; its files deliberately NOT in this commit). OPEN, named: the χ-linearity criterion probe; the no-subaerial-cells precondition (unchanged); multiple beacons + policy; water at fine levels; SRC_HASH does not cover rustc/LLVM version or FMA contraction (sidebar §2 — a `#form-complete-content-addressed-key` question, Joseph's call). `cargo test -p vivarium-world --lib` green: 168 (incl. 3 concurrent explorer-agent watch tests). Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (response_census) · crates/vivarium-world/src/spec.rs (Beacon) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/response_time_probe.rs · crates/vivarium-world/examples/patch_carve_probe.rs · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md (FE8) · core/src/form-manifest-prescribes-vivium.md (FE6) · core/src/obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound.md · ASSUMPTIONS.md · msc/research-lem-sota/lem-time-interior-prior-art-2026-07-28.md · msc/agent-briefs/l13-patch-coords.md · DECISIONS[erosion-settle-history-is-a-materialized-stage-chain] · DECISIONS[erosion-convergence-gate-is-a-measured-no-go-and-the-count-must-not-be-relocated]

|decision[finer-than-a-face-is-a-window-and-the-carve-tier-must-be-counted] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT — the explorer renders finer than L9. Joseph's second question was the brief: "How close are we to something that can zoom in and look at something graphically or at higher detail?" The crate's own comment answered it — "Finer than L9 wants a per-region quadtree, not a bigger monolith" — and a camera-centred WINDOW is that quadtree's first rung: past L9 the six-face globe becomes one 384x384 window into one face, so cost is FLAT in level instead of quadrupling (L13 whole-globe would be 400 M cells; the window is 147 k, under a tenth of the L9 globe it replaces). Not a second renderer: `mesh::FaceInput` gained `(oi, oj, nx)` and a whole face became the special case `oi=oj=0, nx=1<<level`, because a globe path and a region path sharing no meshing code will disagree about the world and the disagreement will look like terrain. Levels now L5-L14. `B` goes to the selected chain's own extent (read from the census, never a typed coordinate) and `G` cycles chains; pressing `E` on a windowed chain goes there automatically, since a scrub of something off-screen is a scrub of a blank planet.
  |reason
    THE FINDING THAT JUSTIFIES THE HUD's NEW LINE, and it is the sharper twin of `#obs-coarse-view-draws-the-uncarved-prior`: asking a region FINER than its level does not fall through. `grid_pos` accepts any `level >= self.level` and `surface_m` answers with a bilinear read of the carve PLUS the fine prior's detail re-added — the fidelity ladder working exactly as designed. So an L13 view over an L9 carve is not blocky and not obviously coarse; it is full of correct, lawful, kilometre-scale relief that NO FLUVIAL RUN AT THAT SCALE PRODUCED. Valleys with no drainage behind them, and a trained eye reads them as fluvial because at that scale that is what fluvial form looks like. The coarse case is caught by one glance at provenance paint; here provenance says "eroded" and is telling the truth. Only the CARVE TIER distinguishes them, and nothing reported it. The HUD now counts drawn cells by the level of the region that answered them and names the coarser fraction loudly.
    A BUG THE PICTURE FOUND, worth recording because reasoning did not find it: my stage predicate was `(src, epochs)` and the cohort grouping was `(src, level)` — the grouping was right and the loader did not follow it. With a global L9 sweep at epochs 5..40 beside the L13 beacon at 10..300, the beacon's early stages loaded the GLOBAL tiles too, so the surround was carved at epoch 10 and reverted to uncarved prior at epoch 50 when the coarse ladder ran out. On screen: terrain around the region changing as you scrub — a coverage cliff wearing the appearance of geology, which is precisely the fault the level constraint exists to prevent. Nothing but the rendered frame said so. That is `#norm-no-depiction-without-referent` FE(2)'s argument for the eye as an instrument, turned on the instrument itself. Fixed to `(src, level, epochs)`; coverage is now constant across the scrub (L13 43%, uncarved prior 57%, every stage).
    TWO SMALLER CALLS. The seam instrument now measures ONLY edges that lie on a face boundary: a window has four edges and up to four of them are ordinary interior cell boundaries, and measuring those reports the world's own terrain steps as chart discontinuities — an instrument manufacturing its own signal and painting magenta over good terrain. And the ghost ring now reads the loaded regions rather than the bare prior: for a whole face the ghosts fall on other faces and the shortcut cost nothing visible, but for a window the ghost ring is ordinary in-face carved terrain, and the prior there would put a one-cell moat of uncarved ground around every patch — a manufactured discontinuity at exactly the scale a fine view exists to inspect.
  |impact  VERIFIED BY LOOKING (the point of the `VIVARIUM_SHOT` idiom, extended with `VIVARIUM_EROSION=<i>` and startup framing so an autoshot of a windowed chain photographs the chain and not a planet): at L13 (~1.2 km/cell) over the beacon, epoch 10 is scattered blobs and epoch 300 is organized lineated ridge-and-valley. That is the first picture in this tree at a scale where fluvial form is a SHAPE rather than a single cell, and it is what Joseph's eye was always the instrument for. `crates/vivarium-explore`: `pull::Patch` + `Patch::centred` (window SLIDES rather than clips at a face edge, so cell scale never silently drops), `mesh::FaceInput{oi,oj,nx}`, per-cell face-global translation named once in the worker, `FrameFacts::{tier_cells, cells}`, `hud::tier_line`, `frame_cohort`, LEVEL_GLOBE_MAX/LEVEL_MAX/PATCH_NX. `watch.rs`: `ErosionCohort` keyed by `(src, level)` with `bounds`/`centre`/`span_cells`/`is_global`, +1 test. Segments to present truth: `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` FE(11) gains the level half of the cohort constraint and the extent-is-census corollary; `#obs-coarse-view-draws-the-uncarved-prior` gains FE(6) (the fine-over-coarse direction, asymmetric and more dangerous) and its Working Note names the concrete cure.
    OPEN, named not attempted: `surface_at` returns a bare `f64`, so the explorer reconstructs the tier by calling `tier_at` per cell alongside it — two traversals where one would do, and a discipline every other consumer must re-invent; a `surface_with_tier -> (f64, Option<u8>)` closes both directions of this observation at once and is builder-side. Pull is ~2.5 s per L13 window frame, so a 30-stage play is ~75 s — steppable, not fluid; no profiling done. No stage-to-stage RATE channel. The window follows the camera centre with no hysteresis, so a small drag past a cell boundary re-meshes. `bin/check` green. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-explore/src/pull.rs (Patch) · crates/vivarium-explore/src/mesh.rs (FaceInput oi/oj/nx) · crates/vivarium-explore/src/hud.rs (tier_line) · crates/vivarium-world/src/watch.rs (ErosionCohort) · core/src/obs-coarse-view-draws-the-uncarved-prior.md (FE6) · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md (FE11) · core/src/form-fidelity-ladder.md · DECISIONS[the-explorer-scrubs-world-time-and-the-cohort-is-half-the-key] · DECISIONS[the-explorer-is-an-instrument-and-the-wall-is-a-handle]

|decision[drainage-is-a-reader-of-a-stage-not-a-store-citizen] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT — `Fluvial::drainage_surface`, the reader that recovers the discharge field the fluvial kernel computes every epoch and throws away. Until now `Fluvial::drainage` existed only inside a live run: nothing reached the store, so no view, probe or segment could say where water flows on this planet. The reader takes a stored stage's heights and returns MFD drainage area, D8 single-receiver accumulation, the D8 receiver tree, the depression-filled surface, per-cell fill depth, and a stats block (subaerial count, land runoff, basin count / largest-basin share / basins-for-half, channel cells at three thresholds under both routers, max-D8-over-max-MFD spread, discharge-weighted MFD out-degree, straight-run length and how much of it sits in filled ground, depression cells / volume / depth). Heights are restored; three tests convict it (restore is bit-exact AND the fill provably moved something first, precip weight measurably moves the trunk, the depression measure fires on a 200 m crater and stays silent on a graded dome).
  |reason
    THE JUDGMENT CALL was memo-versus-recompute, and it went to recompute on the `response_census` precedent (`DECISIONS[the-epoch-count-has-a-derivation-and-the-first-beacon-aims-at-it]`). Drainage is a PURE FUNCTION of a stored stage plus keyed climate — one fill, one receiver pass, one sort, two accumulations over nx² cells — so a memo would need its own nomos version, its own key, and a world rebuild in order to hold numbers the caller can already have for the cost of a store read. `DECISIONS[memoized-means-store-object]` gives the condition that would flip it and it is not met: nothing here is a cold recompute a view pays PER FRAME. Recording the reasoning because the obvious move (persist a discharge field per stage) is the one that also costs the most to undo.
    BOTH ROUTERS ARE RETURNED, and that is the load-bearing design choice rather than a convenience. MFD at p=1.0 is what the kernel erodes with and it is a DIFFUSED field (`#obs-cube-locked-kernel-bias` FE(1) — the fan does not converge); D8 concentrates into threads and carries the grid-alignment artifact MFD was adopted to dissolve. A view that paints one while narrating the other manufactures content, which `#norm-no-depiction-without-referent` forbids, so the reader hands over both and the measured gap between them (`spread_ratio`, `mean_out_degree`) rather than making the choice on the consumer's behalf.
    PRECIP IS AN INPUT, NOT A DEFAULT, and this exposed a live trap: `Fluvial::from_region` rebuilds a field from stored heights alone and leaves `precip_weight` at ones. Every existing reader of a stored region — `response_census` included — is therefore computing UNIFORM-RAIN discharge, which is not what the kernel ran (climate carries fated ±50% low-frequency jitter). Measured at the L13 beacon it is worth under 1%, because the jitter's features are ~1000 km and the patch is 313 km, so the weight is near-constant after normalizing by the tile mean. The gap grows with span; the docs say so and a test guards the wiring.
  |impact  `erosion.rs`: `DrainageSurface`, `DrainageStats`, `CHANNEL_THRESHOLD_CELLS`, `Fluvial::drainage_surface`, 3 tests. `examples/discharge_probe`: seven-part read-only probe over the live world — per-tile census of the L13 beacon patch, uniform-vs-fated rain, all 30 stored stages of one tile, tile-local versus assembled routing, ASCII renders of both routers (log4 ramp in cells-drained; downsample is MAX-POOL and says so, because a mean dissolves the thread it is drawn to show), depression capacity, and the L9 bed the water nomos settles on. Findings landed as `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` (new segment) and a residual row + FE(4) correction in `#detail-erosion-composition`. Coordination: `msc/agent-briefs/drainage-reader-for-the-explorer.md` for the concurrent explorer agent, whose crate is deliberately untouched here. `bin/check` green. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (drainage_surface) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/discharge_probe.rs · core/src/obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins.md · core/src/detail-erosion-composition.md · msc/agent-briefs/drainage-reader-for-the-explorer.md · DECISIONS[memoized-means-store-object] · DECISIONS[the-epoch-count-has-a-derivation-and-the-first-beacon-aims-at-it] · DECISIONS[no-depiction-without-a-world-referent]

|decision[the-tile-edge-is-the-base-level-and-that-is-why-there-are-no-lakes] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  MEASURED, not built — the probe answer to CONSOLIDATION-STATUS open-work item 6 ("zero inland standing water at L9, surfaced by the instrument and wanting a probe"). Across the whole live planet at L9: 384 erosion tiles, 96 with land, 58 013 subaerial cells, and 12 cells in 3 tiles sitting in a closed depression deeper than 1 m — 0.02%. Standing water needs somewhere to stand; the bed has essentially nowhere. The absence is a property of the eroded bed, not a failure of the water kernel, and the question was filed under the wrong system.
  |reason
    THE CAUSE IS THE OUTLET POLICY. `Fluvial::outlets` makes every edge cell a sink on any tile short of a whole cube face — which is every builder tile (64² of a 512² face) and every beacon tile. Priority-Flood then fills to those outlets every epoch and stream-power incises toward them, so each tile grades itself to its own perimeter and finishes with no closed depression at all. That is not a mistake and the code says so in place ("the principled incomplete-tile base level until flux-BC"); what was missing was the price. Measured at the L13 beacon (4×4 tiles, 30 stages): tile-locally, ZERO depression cells; re-route the assembled 256² patch as one field and 8 532 of 65 536 cells (13.0%) are in one, deepest 411 m — so assembling also MANUFACTURES basins where adjacent tiles graded to different perimeter heights, and a depression census on a stitched surface cannot separate geography from seam. Largest trunk goes 3.58e9 m² tile-local to 1.38e10 m² assembled, a 3.85× cut. And across all 30 stages of one tile the largest-basin share oscillates 32.6–45.8% with NO trend while channel counts hold flat: the network is set within ten epochs and only jitters, because base level is fixed 32 cells away and there is no room for capture. The beacon's 300 epochs buy incision depth, not network evolution — which corrects how a stage scrub of it should be described.
    IT COMPOUNDS WITH, IT DOES NOT REPLACE, `#obs-water-fill-never-settles`: 200 steps is 40 s of world time at any level, so a bed WITH depressions would not have filled them either. Two independent sufficient causes; repairing one alone changes nothing on screen, and any plan that fixes only the water side will look like a fix and not be one.
    TWO SMALLER FINDINGS from the same instrument. The straight drainage runs the eye catches on an assembled patch are the Priority-Flood ε-gradient orienting flow across the flats the seam basins create — 80% of long-run cells sit in FILLED ground on the assembled patch versus 0% within a tile, so the ε-fill is a DIRECTIONAL defect as well as the mass mint the residual table already names. And MFD's discharge-weighted out-degree is 1.96–2.30 (flow leaves a typical cell in two directions) while max-D8-over-max-MFD is 0.56–1.01 — the fan spreads along the way and re-converges at the trunk, so a one-cell-wide painted river is a two-cell fan drawn thin.
  |impact  Segment `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` (new, §VII Geosphere — the conviction sits next to the kernel it convicts, `Fluvial::outlets`); `#detail-erosion-composition` gains an edge-sink residual row, the ε-fill row gains its directional half, and FE(4)'s outlet sentence is corrected from reassurance to present truth. Instrument `examples/discharge_probe`. NOT CLAIMED, and named in the segment: the whole-face control (coast-only outlets, the one configuration without edge sinks) is the cheapest sharpening and is untried; nothing is measured at other levels; the remedy (halo-exchanged or flux-BC tile boundaries, `#form-seam-flux-exchange` / `#form-face-flux-register`) is a direction, not a costed plan. The store read was stale-by-src and the probe says so on screen — these are shape claims about surfaces the store really holds, and a rebuild is expected to move digits, not clauses. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (outlets, drainage_surface) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/discharge_probe.rs · core/src/obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins.md · core/src/detail-erosion-composition.md · core/src/obs-water-fill-never-settles.md · CONSOLIDATION-STATUS.md (open-work 6) · DECISIONS[drainage-is-a-reader-of-a-stage-not-a-store-citizen]

|decision[tectonic-structure-is-the-column-not-a-painted-rate] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT — three column-side moves toward Joseph's realism grant, each fated geometry under `#form-isostasy-column`'s council-accepted law (structure from the column read through Airy; never plates-as-state, never a painted rate). (1) SUTURES: where two GROWN cratons overlap (`craton_field_at_tp` w2), crust thickens (+6 km at full overlap ≈ +1 km stand) — collisional orogeny as the overlap of accreting caps. MEASURED: seeds 0/1 and the live world have ZERO suture cells at the present anchor, 130–1125 by Tp 1450–1350 (seed 7 collides early: 738 at present) — orogeny arrives IN TIME, after first emergence, exactly the ladder's order; the present-anchor world honestly has no mountain belts yet. (2) ZONATION: felsic crust thins nucleus→rim (accretion age as geometry), MEAN-PRESERVING about the cap's area-mean radial (≈2/3). (3) THE DRIVER IS THE COLUMN'S DERIVATIVE: uplift v1 = freeboard(Tp−δ)−freeboard(Tp) for one declared scalar (`TP_COOLING_PER_EPOCH_C`, ASSUMPTIONS "cooling per erosion epoch") — SIGNED (ocean floor subsides −0.24..−0.34 m/epoch; land rises med ~0.8, rims/sutures to ~5–8) and zero-mean on the reference grid: rise here IS subsidence there, now in the driver. Retires the v0 fBm texture and the whole-seafloor-rises artifact the explorer's change paint surfaced independently. Versions bumped (uplift-2026-07-28a-column-derivative; lithosphere-2026-07-28a-zonation-and-sutures); UPLIFT deps/consumes now name the real chain.
  |reason
    A REFUTATION IS RECORDED MID-COURSE because it changed the design and the claim. First-cut zonation (subtract z·radial outright) was measured to make integration WORSE at L9 — subaerial −10–20 %, maxcatch 34→20 — a land-area regression wearing a structure improvement's name; corrected to mean-preserving same-day, after which the same instrument reads land +11 %, channelized cells ×4 (39→157), max catchment ×1.5–2.4 (34→81, cohort-to-cohort at current sea). THE LARGER HYPOTHESIS ALSO FAILED HONESTLY: tilting a radial dome does not change drainage TOPOLOGY — radial nets stay radial — so zonation buys longer paths and more land, not integrated basins. Integration's real gates, now named with evidence: (a) COLLISION STRUCTURE, which this work builds but which the present anchor has not reached (the assembled, belted world exists DOWN THE COOLING CHAIN — the principled unlock is era-parameterized erosion, building the fluvial rung against colder stages, which is also the iterated erode→rebound rung FE(9) already names as open); (b) the TILE-EDGE BASE LEVEL, measured concurrently by the discharge strand ( #obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins ): every partial tile grades to its own perimeter, capping any catchment at one tile by construction. Realism honesty for the present anchor: small fragmented radial nets on isolated hot-era cratons are PHYSICALLY TRUTHFUL for a pre-collision early-Abyssal — the world is young, not wrong; what the eye deserves is access to its assembled future.
  |impact  `lithosphere.rs` (CratonField w1/w2/radial; SUTURE_CRUST_M; CRATON_ZONATION mean-preserving; column_at_tp); `uplift.rs` (rewritten: column-derivative rate, signed/zero-mean tests replace the positive-band test); `nomotheke.rs` (UPLIFT + LITHOSPHERE decls to present truth); `erosion.rs` (ResponseCensus gains max_catchment_cells); `examples/response_time_probe.rs` (COHORT-AWARE, never merges src cohorts — the explorer's cohort discipline applied to the census after it was caught reading a merged mush; env COHORT filter). ASSUMPTIONS: "uplift rate" row DELETED (constant gone from code), three new rows (cooling per erosion epoch; suture crustal thickening; craton zonation — the last carrying its own refutation record). Segments to present truth: `#form-isostasy-column` FE(5) (both halves of "orogeny adds later relief" now have fated stand-ins, with the zero-at-present measurement), FE(7) (driver paragraph rewritten), Working Notes modules. Calibration battery GREEN under all three changes: land-fraction bands, monotone emergence, timing verdict + known-bads, craton morphology, rock-mass conservation, zero-mean. OPEN, named: era-parameterized erosion (the assembled world down-chain — the realism unlock this work points at); keel thickening at sutures; the strength limit capping early topography; whether the L13 beacon should move to a suture belt once an era with one is buildable. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/lithosphere.rs (craton_field_at_tp) · crates/vivarium-world/src/uplift.rs · crates/vivarium-world/src/nomotheke.rs · core/src/form-isostasy-column.md (FE5/FE7) · ASSUMPTIONS.md · DECISIONS[isostasy-is-the-uplift-nomos-and-the-keel-is-half-of-it] · DECISIONS[craton-nucleation-growth-built-few-coherent-cratons-replace-fbm-speckle] · DECISIONS[uplift-is-structurally-incapable-of-keeping-its-promise]

|decision[erosions-criterion-is-a-shape-test-and-one-response-time-is-a-quarter-of-it] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT AND MEASURED — the first convergence criterion for the fluvial kernel that can FAIL, and the measurement that says the authored count does not meet it. `Fluvial::chi_profile` integrates Perron & Royden's chi (2013, ESPL 38:570-576) upstream along the kernel's own D8 tree and, beside it, the driven-steady-state profile this kernel's incision update implies. `examples/chi_convergence_probe` reads the L13 beacon's 30-stage settle history out of the store — heights, uplift, climate and recorded residual all under the stage's own `src=`, never the running binary's — and reports per stage how much channel-elevation amplitude chi leaves unexplained, what slope the fit recovers, and what that stage's own mean |dh| was. The shape statistic falls 0.472 (uncarved prior) -> 0.439 (stage 10) -> 0.340 (stage 300), trunk-only to 0.161; carrying the most channelized tile past the ladder shows it reaching ~0.15 (trunks ~0.06) at 1200-1500 epochs and then plateauing. So the beacon's 300 — chosen as one a-priori response time — is roughly a QUARTER of the approach. Segment `#obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion`.
  |reason
    A SHAPE TEST SURVIVES WHAT KILLED THE OBVIOUS ONE. `#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound` measured a |dh| tolerance dead; chi-linearity is a statement about the surface at one instant, so a pinned residual is not an obstacle to it, and a tile with no channel produces NO TEST rather than a spurious pass — the exact inverse of the failure that segment names as the dangerous one. It is also evaluable where the literature's own metrics are not: the fit residual is ~27 m at stage 300 against an f32 resolution of ~1.2e-4 m at kilometre elevations, five orders of margin, where the published steady-state metrics run to 1e-6..1e-14 m and cannot be represented here at all.
    THE SEGMENT IT LEANS ON GETS A CORRECTION, not a softening. FE(4) said the residual "stays near the uplift rate forever rather than falling to zero." Measured on this patch it does neither: it falls from 1.115 to 0.475 m/epoch across 300 epochs against a mean uplift of 0.50, and plateaus near 0.18 (~0.35 U) at 3000. The clause is now stated as what actually blocks a tolerance and it is STRONGER — a worked tile decays to a nonzero floor nobody can compute in advance, an inert tile reads the uplift rate exactly and forever, and the inert value is one a driven tile passes straight through on its way down. The tolerance is unavailable because the two classes straddle every candidate threshold, not because nothing ever moves.
    THE RATE HALF IS A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT A GATE, and saying so is the honest half of the result. The fitted dz/dchi does not converge to the pure incision-uplift prediction U/(k_dt A0^m) — it converges to ~1.6x it. With deposition off the same instrument recovers the prediction to within 15% (lib test), so the offset is the live Davy-Lague term: re-laid sediment must be re-cut, and G=1 brackets the steepening in [1, 1+G] with the measured 1.6 inside it. Until that factor is DERIVED the rate half measures the composition rather than gating it — which also means the two halves are not redundant, since a landscape twice as steep in chi passes the shape half at exactly zero and is caught only by the rate half.
    SENSITIVITY IS STATE-DEPENDENT AND UNFLATTERING, reported at every call rather than assumed ( #norm-probe-sensitivity ). Adding a knickpoint to the MEASURED profile — not to an idealization, because the question is whether a step is visible over the residual already there — the smallest detectable one is ~2 sd (87 m) at stage 300 and ~1 sd (34 m) at 3000. Below that the criterion is saturated by its own departure. The statistic also improves monotonically with the channelization threshold (0.370/0.340/0.252/0.161/0.066 at 3/10/30/100/300 median cell areas), which is the stream-power law's domain showing through: headwaters are creep- and talus-governed and should not be chi-linear.
  |impact  `erosion.rs`: `ChiProfile`, `Fluvial::chi_profile`, `Fluvial::uplift_rate` accessor, 2 tests (the surface approaches the predicted profile and the fitted slope recovers U/(k_dt A0^m); the criterion rejects a knickpoint while a doubled slope passes the shape half). `examples/chi_convergence_probe` — read-only, cohort-disciplined, and it computes past the ladder's end without storing anything. Segments: `#obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion` new (§VII Geosphere); `#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound` FE(4) restated to present truth, FE(6) and its Working Note updated from "unbuilt" to built-with-no-threshold. NOT CLAIMED, and named in the segment: no threshold is declared (its converged value is a property of the composition and nothing derives it); the deposition attribution rests on two configurations, not a G sweep; one seed, one patch, one level, 16 tiles; and the uniform-U premise that lets FE(3)-(5) be read as being about the composition holds for THIS cohort only — the uplift nomos has since become the column's signed derivative, and re-running after that rebuild is the obvious next measurement. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (chi_profile) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/chi_convergence_probe.rs · core/src/obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion.md · core/src/obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound.md · core/OUTLINE.md · msc/research-lem-sota/lem-time-interior-prior-art-2026-07-28.md (§1.4, §4.3) · msc/agent-briefs/l13-patch-coords.md · DECISIONS[erosion-convergence-gate-is-a-measured-no-go-and-the-count-must-not-be-relocated] · DECISIONS[the-epoch-count-has-a-derivation-and-the-first-beacon-aims-at-it] · DECISIONS[the-tile-edge-is-the-base-level-and-that-is-why-there-are-no-lakes]

|decision[the-chi-criterions-residual-floor-is-deposition-and-the-rate-half-is-not-derivable-from-g] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  MEASURED — the re-run of `examples/chi_convergence_probe` against the world rebuilt under `uplift-2026-07-28a-column-derivative`, plus the deposition sweep the previous entry left as named-open. Two results, one positive and one negative, and the negative is the one that changes what the criterion can be used for. POSITIVE: at G = 0 the chi criterion is EXACT on real terrain — shape residual 0.009 and the fitted dz/dchi recovers U/(k_dt A0^m) to three digits over six tiles. So the ~0.15 residual floor is not a limit of the criterion or of the instrument; DEPOSITION carries all of it. NEGATIVE: across G = 0/0.25/0.5/1/2 the offset is monotone and tight (per-tile spreads do not overlap between rows) but does NOT track 1+G — ratio/(1+G) declines 1.00 -> 0.914 -> 0.877 -> 0.773 -> 0.751. The Davy-Lague reading is refuted, the rate half stays a diagnostic of the composition rather than a gate on the landscape, and erosion does not get a full declared criterion this cycle.
  |reason
    THE "QUARTER OF THE APPROACH" NUMBER SURVIVED THE DRIVER CHANGE, which is what makes it worth keeping. All four L13 cohorts now in the store are measured — two under each uplift nomos, each read under its own `src=` — and the new driver's chain runs 1-R²(√) 0.508 -> 0.288 over 300 stages against a converged ~0.13 reached at 750-1500 epochs. Better than the old cohort's 0.439 -> 0.340, same conclusion.
    THE DISCRIMINATION THIS SEGMENT PREDICTS DID NOT GET ITS TEST, and reporting that is the point of the re-run. FE(2)'s zero-parameter form exists because it survives a spatially varying U where the literature form does not. The expectation going in was that within-tile sigma/|mean| on land would rise well above the 0.21 measured on the old cohort. MEASURED IT FELL TO 0.002 — the column-derivative driver is coherent at CONTINENT wavelength and the beacon patch spans 313 km, so inside one 78 km tile it is very nearly constant, and no channel cell sits on non-positive uplift. The premise was not stressed; the two forms were never asked to disagree. That is a fact about the FOOTPRINT, not the driver, and the fix is a wider footprint or a coarser level, not a different nomos. Recorded because the opposite conclusion — "measured under a varying driver, still works" — was available and would have been false.
    THE SWEEP WAS WIDENED MID-RESULT and the first answer was discarded. One tile gave a G = 1 row well off its neighbours (ratio 1.38 where the trend wanted ~1.5, with an anomalous trunk statistic beside it) — a basin capture landing where it landed. Six tiles with per-tile spread printed made the curve monotone and the spreads non-overlapping. A trend read from the single-tile version would have been a trend read from noise, and the printed spread is what lets the next reader check that it is not.
    ONE REGULARITY IS RECORDED AND DELIBERATELY NOT PROMOTED: ratio/(1+G/2) holds within 1.00-1.13 across the same five rows. It is five points with no derivation. The candidate mechanism is that only part of the eroded volume is re-laid inside the network while the rest reaches an outlet and is lost to the sea — unverified. It is labelled an observed regularity in the segment AND in the probe's own printed output, because a number this tidy is exactly the kind that gets cited as a law by the third reader.
  |impact  `examples/chi_convergence_probe` restructured: measures EVERY longest chain rather than picking one (a silent pick is how a cohort comparison becomes an accident, and the store holds chains under two drivers), labels each cohort by the uplift nomos riding in its own key, reports the literature and zero-parameter forms side by side, and gained the multi-tile deposition sweep. `CHI_SRC` pins a cohort; no lib change, so the store is untouched. Segment `#obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion` to present truth: FE(3) is now a two-driver table, FE(5) is the sweep with the G = 0 exactness result, FE(6) is the new negative, FE(10) states the untested-premise finding, and the Working Notes retire the sweep as open while opening the footprint question. `#obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound` FE(4) gains the second driver's floor. OPEN: a derivation of the steepening for detachment-plus-deposition (what would make the rate half a gate); a footprint that stresses the varying-U premise; other seeds and levels. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/examples/chi_convergence_probe.rs · core/src/obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion.md · core/src/obs-erosion-residual-is-driver-bound.md · crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (chi_profile) · crates/vivarium-world/src/uplift.rs · DECISIONS[erosions-criterion-is-a-shape-test-and-one-response-time-is-a-quarter-of-it] · DECISIONS[tectonic-structure-is-the-column-not-a-painted-rate]

|decision[two-claims-distill-the-days-pattern-boundary-contracts-and-mechanisms-over-disciplines] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  TWO NEW SEGMENTS under Joseph's highest-leverage-long-term grant, each distilling measured 2026-07-28 evidence into citable law rather than leaving it scattered across three strands' observations. (1) `#form-declared-boundary-contract` (§II, Critical): the flux web types QUANTITIES but not DOMAINS — erosion-tile passes every flux audit while its edge policy caps every basin at one tile, manufactures 13% seam pits, and un-ponds the planet (all measured, #obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins). The cure follows box ③'s accepted pattern: a boundary-contract column on domain-bearing promises, `Undeclared` as the honest first value, tile↔tile seams recognized as seams (#form-seam-flux-exchange applied at same-level). Grain boundaries generalize it: tile↔tile, pour↔tile, patch↔world — the day's three largest defects, one shape, now one owned claim. (2) `#norm-caught-disciplines-become-mechanisms` (§I, Critical): the day's cleanest correlation — every mechanism held (refusing store handle, compile-included ledger, determinism lint, demand-is-not-identity test), every near-miss was a discipline (cohort merging ×3, stale quoted numbers, arithmetic-not-measurement) — stated as an obligation with an incident-earned license (no speculative scaffolding), plus the pre-registration clause, the capability-non-claims clause, and the three-part machine-facing fence (named / conviction-fenced / retirement-path) that today's clean expedients all satisfied.
  |reason  Both are the kind of claim that otherwise gets rediscovered expensively: the next agent who wonders why the flux audit is green while basins are capped, and the next session that restates a discipline after its failure instead of converting it. Writing them as segments (not SOP prose) keeps them in the sole claim channel with honest status (`robust-qualitative`, unratified, evidence checkable via the day's DECISIONS rows) and revisit conditions (the norm's FE(7) counterweight: mechanisms are earned by incidents, and a proposal without an incident argues against the norm, not from it).
  |impact  NEW core/src/form-declared-boundary-contract.md + core/src/norm-caught-disciplines-become-mechanisms.md + two OUTLINE rows. Scheduled from the norm's FE(2): cohort-safe reads as code (this session); measurement-provenance as convention (hardens if caught failing). The boundary-contract schema field lands with or after the cross-tile base-level work, not as a blocker. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref core/src/form-declared-boundary-contract.md · core/src/norm-caught-disciplines-become-mechanisms.md · core/src/obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins.md · core/OUTLINE.md · DECISIONS[tectonic-structure-is-the-column-not-a-painted-rate] · DECISIONS[the-chi-criterions-residual-floor-is-deposition-and-the-rate-half-is-not-derivable-from-g]

|decision[the-per-cell-chi-form-earns-its-place-and-the-tile-edge-is-what-hides-it] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  MEASURED — the wide-footprint run that decides whether `chi_profile`'s zero-parameter form buys what its algebra says. It does: on a network whose channels cross the driver's gradient the per-cell form leaves **1.3-1.5x less** of the channel profile's amplitude unexplained than chi does (1.44-1.89x on a second face). And the reason nothing in the live tree could show it is now named: the driver is coherent at CONTINENT wavelength while the outlet policy caps every basin far below that wavelength, so a channel never crosses a gradient. Two facts compose into one blind spot and neither alone would cause it.
  |reason
    THE COMPARISON HAD TO BE REBUILT BEFORE IT MEANT ANYTHING. The zero-parameter column carries FE(5)'s deposition steepening, which the free-slope literature form absorbs — so head-to-head it was losing on an offset, not on shape, and any verdict from it would have been an artifact of counting parameters unfairly. The fair form regresses z on the predicted profile with a free intercept and scale: TWO fitted parameters, exactly as many as the literature form. It also comes with its own null check, because under a spatially invariant U the predicted profile is an affine function of chi and the two must be THE SAME FIT. Measured at epoch 0 of the whole-face run: ratio 1.003 at an along-channel U span of 1.02. The instrument passes the test that would have convicted it.
    THE PREMISE STATISTIC WAS BEING MEASURED ON THE SEABED, and this is the correction worth recording. `sigma/|mean|` was medianed over every tile in the sweep — 377 of 384 of which produce no test — so it reported the ocean floor's uniformity as though it were the premise under the channels. Restricted to the tiles that actually produce a fit, the L9 driver's within-tile variation is **0.242, not 0.000**. The number moved by two orders of magnitude and it moved the reading with it: the driver DOES vary across an L9 tile. What does not vary is U along a fitted channel — median max/min **1.015** — because no basin can exceed one tile ( `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` ) and within a tile the basins are a small fraction of it. Without that correction the finding would have been "the driver is too smooth", which is false and would have pointed the fix at the uplift nomos instead of at the tile boundary.
    THE EXPERIMENT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A WORLD CLAIM. A whole-face tile is the one configuration `Fluvial::outlets` gives coast-only base levels, so assembling the landiest L9 face from its 64 stored tiles and running the kernel on it as one 512² field produces basins that CAN cross the gradient. The builder never ran that network, assembling stored tiles manufactures seam basins, and the run is in memory and never stored — all three said in the segment, in the probe's printed output, and here, because a number this favourable is exactly the kind that gets quoted without its scope.
    AN INSTRUMENT WART, RECORDED RATHER THAN QUIETLY FIXED: the median along-channel U span is not the right companion to an amplitude-pooled residual ratio. At 500 epochs the median basin sees a span of 1.029 while the ratio is already 1.42, because pooling is amplitude-weighted and the large gradient-crossing basins dominate it. The two columns still move together across the run, so the reading holds, but a future tightening should weight the span the way the residual is weighted.
  |impact  `examples/chi_convergence_probe`: matched-parameter U-form column throughout, along-channel U-span per basin, driver statistics restricted to tiles that produce a test, and `whole_face_discrimination` (assemble a face, run it, watch the two forms part company) behind `CHI_FACE` / `CHI_FACE_EPOCHS`. Read-only and no lib change, so the store is untouched. Segment `#obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion`: FE(10) is now the three-footprint table with the blind-spot mechanism, FE(11) the whole-face result with its scope; Epistemic Status and Falsifiers extended; Working Notes retire the untested-premise item and open the amplitude-weighting one. OPEN: a builder footprint whose basins are not capped — which is the seam work ( `#form-seam-flux-exchange` / `#form-face-flux-register` ), not this segment's; other seeds and faces. THE PRACTICAL CONSEQUENCE worth carrying: until a tile boundary stops being a base level, the per-cell form is strictly better and strictly invisible, and any decision that picks between the two forms on live-tree evidence will pick wrong for a structural reason. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/examples/chi_convergence_probe.rs · core/src/obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion.md · core/src/obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins.md · crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (chi_profile, outlets) · DECISIONS[the-chi-criterions-residual-floor-is-deposition-and-the-rate-half-is-not-derivable-from-g] · DECISIONS[the-tile-edge-is-the-base-level-and-that-is-why-there-are-no-lakes] · DECISIONS[tectonic-structure-is-the-column-not-a-painted-rate]

|decision[erosion-at-a-cooling-stage-forks-on-the-ledger-not-on-cost] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  DESIGN + MEASURED, no build. The fluvial rung is the one place the world is pinned to a single moment: its seed surface is `gen::initial_topography_m` = `tectonic_surface_m` at `MANTLE_TP_C`, and `Fluvial::outlets` plus two further kernel reads take `derived_sea_level_m(seed)` with no stage argument — while the whole column stack around it is `_at_tp` parameterized end to end. Parameterizing erosion by the cooling stage is therefore one coordinate, and it lands as TWO segments: `#obs-the-erodible-world-is-down-the-cooling-chain` (what is actually down there, measured) and `#form-erosion-at-a-cooling-stage` (the design). The headline is that the two available compositions — carve each stage independently (A) vs carry a bed forward between stages (B) — are NOT a cost trade. (B) is the iterated erode->rebound->re-erode rung `#form-isostasy-column` FE(9) already names as its own open successor, and it would carve surfaces from which the pour-grain ledger has already subtracted a stage-dependent maturity step. RECOMMENDED FIRST BUILD: one cold stage, stage-parallel, whole-world level — a strict prefix that needs no ledger decision, no retention policy, and no cohort-reader change beyond one selector field.
  |reason
    THE LADDER'S PREMISE HOLDS AND THREE OF ITS ASSUMPTIONS DO NOT, which is why the measurements landed as their own segment rather than as a paragraph in the design. Present anchor -> canonical cold end (Tp 1550 -> 1464.2, seeds 0/1/7 at level 7, 48^2 per face): land 5.27/4.61/6.31% -> 14.40/15.54/18.66%, peak stand 1934/1509/2251 -> 3048/2976/3641 m, subaerial relief s.d. 347/274/414 -> 434/384/559 m. Real target, never carved. But (1) EMERGENCE SATURATES INSIDE THE CHAIN — continuing to 1.0 Ga adds only ~2.9 further points over 1.6 Ga against ~9-12 points over the preceding 0.6, an 8x rate slowdown, so extending `COOL_END_UGA` is not the win and the work is a SELECTION within the authored chain. (2) THE SUTURE BELTS ARE THIN — 0.16/0.46/2.82% of the globe at the cold end, i.e. roughly 1-15% of land; the payoff is more land under weather, not an orogen to dissect, and a design justified by mountain belts would be justified by the smallest of the three effects. (3) THE DRIVER WEAKENS AS THE TARGET GROWS — median uplift over land 0.823 -> 0.650 -> 0.550 m/step, max 7.438 -> 3.060 -> 1.516, so the erosion-uplift balance differs at every stage (the a-priori response time does NOT, being uplift-independent at n=1, so one authored epoch count survives; what moves is the equilibrium relief it approaches).
    THE LEDGER OVERLAP IS THE FINDING THAT REORDERS THE WORK. Mean stand the pour-grain rock-mass ledger has already removed, over pre-ledger land: 76 m at the present anchor, 270 m at the canonical cold end, 491 m by 1.0 Ga — against a subaerial relief s.d. of 347-563 m. Between a fifth and a whole standard deviation, and the share GROWS along the chain. The drop is a pure function of Tp applied fresh per stage rather than accumulated, so nothing compounds within the ledger today; what it means is that (B) has a precondition (A) does not — drive from the PRE-ledger column (`tectonic_surface_pre_ledger_at_tp`, which is already what `uplift.rs` differences) and take over the ledger's mass-return role, or leave the ledger and accept a declared double count. At one stage the two are indistinguishable because there is no second moment at which to check whether they agree; a chain supplies that moment, so the composition that makes the world's history visible is the same one that makes the double count visible.
    KEYING: Tp BITS, NEVER A STAGE INDEX. `epoch-reduction` already keys on `tp_bits` and erosion takes the same field. An index would be under-keying, because `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` FE(9) makes the chain nested by bisection — stage i of refinement k is stage 2i of refinement k+1 — so an index is a function of the requested density rather than of the world, and two builds at different densities would collide on index and serve each other's terrain. Cost stated plainly: this rekeys the whole tile layer (initial-topography, uplift, erosion, water) once, which is the over-key-never-under-key trade taken deliberately and is bounded by the fact that any `src/` edit already invalidates the store through SRC_HASH.
    DEMAND VS IDENTITY, BY THE STATED MEMBERSHIP TEST. A bed at (Tp, epochs) holds the same bytes however many other stages were requested, so the SET of stages a world carves belongs in the demand bucket beside `frames` and `erosion_epochs` (default: the present anchor, so every existing manifest opens as the world it always was), while the individual Tp belongs in the key. Under (B) the set is not fully free: a carried bed depends on its predecessor by complete key, so demand may choose where a walk stops, never which interior stages are skipped.
    THE COHORT QUESTION HAS TWO DIFFERENT ANSWERS AND THAT IS THE CLEANEST WAY TO SEE THE FORK. Under (A) the cooling stage is a COHORT field in `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` FE(11)'s sense — two beds at different Tp are independent carvings, and scrubbing epochs across them would paint the column's evolution as fluvial work, which is exactly the failure FE(11) names for `src`. Under (B) the pair (Tp, epochs) IS the time-index, monotone along the walk, and a scrub across stages is the world evolving. The picture the ladder wants is available only from the composition that pays for it.
    A NEGATIVE RESULT KEPT AT ITS MEASURED SIZE. Threading a stage without its waterline misclassifies only 0.0-0.62% of cells (derived sea moves ~150 m across the whole chain while relief is several hundred). It is a correctness item located at the coast, not a magnitude one, and saying so is part of the claim — the opposite guess is the natural one and would have justified more machinery than the error warrants.
    THE CLOCK IS NOW OVER-DETERMINED, WHICH IS A FEATURE PROVIDED NOBODY PRETENDS IT IS PINNED. The canonical chain spans 176.5 C, i.e. 17.7 C per 0.1 Ga gap; at the declared delta = 0.05 C/epoch that is 353 erosion epochs per stage gap and 3530 for the whole chain, and delta read as a clock is 2.675e5 yr/epoch against the ASSUMPTIONS `epoch <-> years` nominal of 100 — a factor of 2675. Both rows are declared arbitrary and neither is wrong on its own terms; what a composed chain removes is the freedom to hold both. Nothing simulated moves when the label changes (relief is set by U/K per epoch), so there is no contradiction YET — the third claim on the number arrives when K is calibrated against real denudation rates, which the ASSUMPTIONS tuning row already defers until `epoch <-> years` is pinned.
  |impact  TWO NEW SEGMENTS (OUTLINE VII Geosphere): `#obs-the-erodible-world-is-down-the-cooling-chain` (observation/empirical — FE(2) target, FE(3) saturation, FE(4) suture thinness, FE(5) driver decline, FE(6) ledger overlap, FE(7) waterline error, FE(8) clock) and `#form-erosion-at-a-cooling-stage` (formulation/sketch — FE(2) keying, FE(3) chain-over-chain, FE(4)-(5) the fork, FE(6) cohort, FE(7) demand, FE(8) waterline, FE(9) retention, FE(10) recommended first build with its declared deficiency stated up front: E epochs of dissection on a cold column, NOT the landscape that walked there). PRESENT-TRUTH EDITS: `#detail-erosion-composition` FE(4) now records that the rung is pinned to the present anchor and where; `#form-isostasy-column` FE(9)'s open-rung list now carries the measured overlap and names the iterated chain a candidate REPLACEMENT rather than a neighbour; `#form-time-indexed-stage-chains` gains a Working Note for the composition (no FE clause amended — nothing is built). PROBE: `examples/era_erosion_feasibility_probe` (read-only, opens no store, writes nothing). NO CODE CHANGE — `erosion.rs` / `query.rs` / builder plumbing were owned by a concurrent spike tonight and are untouched. OPEN and named: the (A)/(B) choice, which is half `#form-isostasy-column` FE(9)'s; which Tp a default world should carve; a timed cold-stage sweep, the one measurement FE(10)'s cost claim rests on and does not have. Design verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed, no Joseph row.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/examples/era_erosion_feasibility_probe.rs · core/src/obs-the-erodible-world-is-down-the-cooling-chain.md · core/src/form-erosion-at-a-cooling-stage.md · core/src/form-isostasy-column.md (FE9) · core/src/detail-erosion-composition.md (FE4) · core/src/form-time-indexed-stage-chains.md (FE8, FE11) · core/OUTLINE.md · CONSOLIDATION-STATUS.md · msc/agent-briefs/cooling-stage-erosion-meets-cross-tile-base-level.md · ASSUMPTIONS.md · DECISIONS[tectonic-structure-is-the-column-not-a-painted-rate] · DECISIONS[erosion-settle-history-is-a-materialized-stage-chain] · DECISIONS[the-explorer-scrubs-world-time-and-the-cohort-is-half-the-key] · DECISIONS[the-tile-edge-is-the-base-level-and-that-is-why-there-are-no-lakes]

|decision[the-fill-not-the-tile-edge-is-what-leaves-the-world-without-a-lake] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics :supersedes the-tile-edge-is-the-base-level-and-that-is-why-there-are-no-lakes
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  MEASURED, and it is a partial NO-GO against the mechanism the ladder's item 1 was built on. `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` named its own missing control — carve a whole face with coast-only outlets and see whether the closed depressions come back. They do not. They go DOWN: a whole-face carve holds fewer closed depressions than any tiled carve of the same geography, and the count falls monotonically as the tile grain grows (L9 f0, grains 8→512: 503, 335, 185, 51, 48, 11, 2; replicated on f1 and f4 and at L11, where 27798→260). The cause of the missing lakes is not the boundary contract. It is that `Fluvial::erode` keeps Priority-Flood's raise: the three readers that call the same fill (`drainage_surface`, `chi_profile`, `response_census`) each save `self.h` and restore, and the epoch loop does not, so the bed the store receives is depression-free BY CONSTRUCTION. The uncarved prior holds 2.65% of its L9 land (6.41% at L11) in closed depressions — 2.2e13 m3 of capacity per face, deepest 688 m — and ONE epoch leaves 0.12%, at both levels, from both starting densities. What survives after that is a dynamic equilibrium of 3–60 cells that deposition and creep re-create and the next fill erases.
  |reason
    THE CONTROL HAD TO BE A CARVE, NOT AN ASSEMBLY, and that is the whole design. Assembling stored tiles and re-routing them (`discharge_probe` Part 4, and the chi strand's whole-face run) drops the seams in the READER while the beds were still carved against edge sinks; it measures what a viewer sees. Carving a whole face as one field takes `Fluvial::outlets`' full-face branch and measures what the WORLD would have been. Only the second can distinguish a defect the contract causes from one it merely sits beside, and the distinction is exactly where the attribution was wrong.
    WHAT THE REPAIR STILL BUYS, STATED HONESTLY, because the no-go is partial and the item stays at the top of the ladder. It un-starves the tile-local incision driver (the 3.85x stands — it is a property of the carve, and it reaches the bed as 1.96x in rate because incision takes A^0.5). It removes the seam pits, whose fraction is now measured as a monotone function of grain rather than at one point. It supplies the capture-capable base level the chi criterion's form selection needs. It does NOT put water anywhere.
    THE TRUNK STATISTIC CANNOT ADJUDICATE THE CAP, and saying so is more useful than a number would be. Over grains from 39 km to 10008 km, two levels and two epoch counts, no ordering in the grain survives: at L11 a tile six times narrower than the basin suppresses the assembled trunk only to 0.76x while a 156 km tile RAISES it to 2.2x; at L9/300 the whole-face control is neither the largest nor the smallest value in its row. The reason is that the tiling acts in two opposing directions at once — starving basins through the edge sinks, merging them through the seam pits the reader's fill has to flatten. What is clean is the SEAM STEP: mean |dh| across tile boundaries over mean |dh| elsewhere runs 0.03–0.05 above the uncarved prior's null at 40 epochs and 0.21 above it at 300, and across the 300-epoch sweep it peaks exactly at the grain the metric's own boundaries are drawn on, which is the measure convicting itself.
    ONE MECHANISM CLAIM IS NOW KNOWN NOT TO GENERALIZE. `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` FE(6) read the beacon's flat network as "base level fixed 32 cells away leaves no room for capture." Running 300 epochs at L9 under the SAME policy grows the trunk 2.4x, with the whole-face control tracking within 10% at every rung — so the policy does not by itself prevent capture. FE(6) may still be right at L13, where a tile is 78 km rather than 1251 km; it has simply never been run against its own alternative, and CANNOT BE, because no sub-face window can be carved coast-only today. The contract is inferred from `origin == (0,0) && nx == 2^level` rather than declared. That is `#form-declared-boundary-contract`'s missing column showing up as a blocked experiment rather than as a schema complaint, and it is the concrete argument for landing the declaration WITH the repair rather than after it.
  |impact  NEW SEGMENT `#obs-fill-writes-itself-into-the-bed` (observation/exact, OUTLINE VII Geosphere, beside the kernel it convicts). REPLACEMENTS, not softenings: `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` loses its no-lakes attribution in title, lede and FE(3) (FE numbering preserved — three other segments cite it by number), its "whole-face control untried" Working Note is resolved and deleted, and its FE(6) forward note now carries the non-generalization; `#form-declared-boundary-contract` FE(1) loses "the no-lakes fact is a boundary-condition fact" and FE(4) gains the carve-versus-reroute probe distinction plus the blocked-experiment argument; `#detail-erosion-composition` gains a kept-fill residual row and its step (2) now records the non-restore. CONSOLIDATION-STATUS item 1 rewritten with what the repair does and does not buy, plus a new 1b for the fill. NEW INSTRUMENT `examples/base_level_probe` — store-free and world-free (topography, uplift and precipitation jitter are pure functions of seed and cell, so it opens nothing and writes nothing), sweeps the grain at fixed geography, ~2.6 s for a whole L9 face. NOT CLAIMED: one seed; L13/beacon regime untested; the fill repair (route on the filled surface, incise on the unfilled one) is named, is standard in the LEM literature, is a real physics change rather than a bug fix, and is neither built nor costed. Two pre-existing lint defects in touched files fixed in passing. `bin/check` green. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/examples/base_level_probe.rs · crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (erode vs drainage_surface/chi_profile/response_census) · core/src/obs-fill-writes-itself-into-the-bed.md · core/src/obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins.md · core/src/form-declared-boundary-contract.md · core/src/detail-erosion-composition.md · core/OUTLINE.md · CONSOLIDATION-STATUS.md · msc/agent-briefs/cooling-stage-erosion-meets-cross-tile-base-level.md · DECISIONS[the-tile-edge-is-the-base-level-and-that-is-why-there-are-no-lakes] · DECISIONS[two-claims-distill-the-days-pattern-boundary-contracts-and-mechanisms-over-disciplines]

|decision[the-boundary-contract-becomes-nameable-and-the-beacon-answers-differently-from-the-planet] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT, small and deliberately incomplete — `EdgeContract::{BaseLevelSink, NoFluxWall}` on `Fluvial`, naming the two policies `Fluvial::outlets` had been inferring from geometry. `Fluvial::inferred_edge_contract` reproduces that inference verbatim and is the constructor default, so no world changes and a test says so; `set_edge_contract` lets any window be carved under either. It is a KERNEL parameter and NOT a key field, and that line is the point: nothing reached through `query.rs` can select a contract, so the builder's complete key stays complete. Declaring and keying are separate obligations and this is only the first — the key gains the column on the day a keyed caller can choose, and a contract that makes a tile depend on its neighbours has to fold that dependency in too.
  |reason
    THE DECLARATION WAS BUILT BECAUSE A MEASUREMENT WAS BLOCKED, not because a schema felt incomplete. `#obs-fill-writes-itself-into-the-bed` found the tile grain nearly irrelevant at the L9 planet build and said so, but could not test the regime the whole repair is justified by — the beacon, at 78 km tiles — because no sub-face window could be carved without edge sinks. That is `#form-declared-boundary-contract`'s missing column appearing as an experiment nobody can run, which is a better argument for it than any schema complaint.
    AND THE BEACON ANSWERS THE OPPOSITE WAY, which is why it was worth the build. `examples/beacon_contract_probe` carves the L13 footprint (f1, 640/5376, 256^2, 313 km) both ways for 300 epochs and reads both through one NoFluxWall reader. Sixteen tiles hold 39688 of 65536 cells (60.6%) in closed depressions, capacity 1.10e13 m3, sustained across the whole history (40216 at 50 epochs, 39688 at 300); the single-window carve holds 0 to 15. The two beds end 274–297 m apart in mean |dh|, rising monotonically from ~200 m at 50 epochs, and the gap is NOT concentrated at the boundary (296.6 m on the tile rings, 273.8 m inside them) — at this grain the contract restructures the whole window. At L9 the same comparison agrees within 10% at every rung. The contract's consequence is a strong function of grain against geography, not a constant, and both numbers had to exist before either could be read correctly.
    THE 13% WAS AN UNDERSTATEMENT FOR A REASON WORTH RECORDING. `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` FE(5) re-routed the assembled 256^2 patch and found 13.0% of it in closed depressions — but a 256^2 window at L13 is itself a partial tile, so THAT READER HAD A PERIMETER RING OF SINKS. With a declared NoFluxWall reader the same carve reads 60.6%. The instrument had the defect it was measuring, and only a declared contract could take it out.
    NEITHER CONTRACT IS RIGHT AT THE BEACON, and that is the finding rather than a caveat. The window is fully subaerial — 65536 of 65536 cells above derived sea — so BaseLevelSink surrounds it with an outlet ring that exists nowhere in the world while NoFluxWall leaves it with no outlet at all and falls back to making its lowest cell one. A patch with no coast has no honest base level that is a function of the patch. Only a boundary exchanging with the neighbours it actually has can supply one, which is `#form-seam-flux-exchange` reaching the place it is load-bearing.
    ONE METRIC WAS WITHDRAWN RATHER THAN PUBLISHED. An absolute trench depth (mean interior height minus mean perimeter-ring height, per arm) read -101 m on the tiled carve, which no mechanism I could name predicts — the uplift step pins `is_edge` cells under EVERY contract, the prior's own ring-versus-interior offset is already -5 m by accident of terrain, and a direct single-tile check (edge +13.8 m, centre +65.6 m over 300 epochs) contradicted the aggregate's sign. It was replaced by the difference BETWEEN the arms, which has none of those confounds. The withdrawn version is documented in the probe so nobody re-adds it naively.
  |impact  `erosion.rs`: `EdgeContract`, `Fluvial::{inferred_edge_contract, set_edge_contract, edge_contract}`, `outlets` reads the field instead of re-deriving `full_face`, and the no-coast fallback now covers any walled domain rather than only a whole face. THREE NEW TESTS: the inference is preserved exactly (five geometries plus a constructed field); the two contracts carve measurably different worlds with the disagreement largest at the boundary they describe (`#norm-declaration-must-convict`); and — replacing an assertion that encoded the very expectation the day's measurements had refuted — ONE EPOCH ERASES A 200 m CRATER UNDER EITHER CONTRACT, which is the tripwire for the fill repair (`#norm-caught-disciplines-become-mechanisms`): when it fails, the bed can hold water and the segments saying it cannot are due for replacement, and the test says so in its own message. NEW INSTRUMENT `examples/beacon_contract_probe`, store-free like its sibling. Segment updates: `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` FE(5) gains the honest-reader 60.6%, the 280 m arm gap, and the neither-contract-is-right clause (FE NUMBERING PRESERVED — three segments cite it by number); `#obs-fill-writes-itself-into-the-bed` scope corrected from "beacon unmeasured" to "measured, and it goes the other way"; `#form-declared-boundary-contract` FE(2) and Epistemic Status record what is now built versus still argued; `#detail-erosion-composition` FE(4) names the enum; OUTLINE and CONSOLIDATION item 1 carry the grain-dependence. NOT CLAIMED: the key field, the halo, the `Undeclared` schema value, and any contract beyond the two that already existed. `bin/check` green. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (EdgeContract, outlets, 3 tests) · crates/vivarium-world/examples/beacon_contract_probe.rs · crates/vivarium-world/examples/base_level_probe.rs · core/src/form-declared-boundary-contract.md · core/src/obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins.md · core/src/obs-fill-writes-itself-into-the-bed.md · core/src/detail-erosion-composition.md · core/OUTLINE.md · CONSOLIDATION-STATUS.md · DECISIONS[the-fill-not-the-tile-edge-is-what-leaves-the-world-without-a-lake]

|decision[the-fill-becomes-a-routing-surface-and-the-bed-keeps-its-holes] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic physics :supersedes the-fill-not-the-tile-edge-is-what-leaves-the-world-without-a-lake
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  BUILT and MEASURED. `Fluvial::erode` now treats Priority-Flood as a routing surface and subtracts the raise before talus and creep, so the stored bed keeps its closed basins. `fill_depressions` returns the TRUE spill depth — the running max of ORIGINAL heights along the flood path, carried beside the heap key and never ordering it, so routing is bit-identical — which is exactly zero on a flat and therefore never mistakes the epsilon for standing water; no depth threshold in metres was needed. Measured, whole-face L9 f0, same instrument and same prior as the superseded row: the prior's 671 closed-depression cells (2.65% of land, 2.182e13 m3, deepest 688 m) decline 613/571/509/465/441/397/377/357 over epochs 1–8 and settle at 134 cells (0.51%, deepest 635 m) at 40 epochs and 228 (0.47%, deepest 212 m) at 300. The superseded composition read 31 cells (0.12%) at epoch 1 and 2 at epoch 40 from the identical prior. The falsifier that row named for its own central clause — "a gradual decline over the first eight epochs rather than a collapse at epoch 1" — is what the table now is, because the mechanism it described has been removed.
  |reason
    THREE PHYSICS DECISIONS WERE FORCED AND ARE DECLARED RATHER THAN DEFAULTED, and they are where the work actually was. Restoring the raise is four lines; deciding what a lake IS took the rest. (1) A submerged cell does not incise — E = K A^m S^n has no channel slope under standing water — while a subaerial cell draining INTO a lake keeps its term and takes its receiver's height from the lake surface, which is the base level a river entering a lake sees. (2) Sediment arriving at a submerged cell settles into the BED up to the remaining capacity and the surplus spills down the same tree the water does: trapping efficiency 1, declared as a sign-definite unphysical term, mass-conserving, with the error in the RATE of infill rather than the budget. (3) Talus and creep run on the ROCK surface, because they redistribute rock and a water level is not a material surface — running them on the filled surface would silently make the fill's manufactured rock real. None of these questions existed while the fill was kept; the old composition answered all three implicitly with "there are no lakes." That is the ordinary shape of a defect that removes a degree of freedom: it looks like a bug and is also a modelling decision nobody made.
    THE TRIPWIRE FIRED ALONE AND ITS MESSAGE WAS RIGHT. `one_epoch_erases_a_crater_under_either_contract`, written the same day by an agent who could not land the repair, failed 1-of-177 with a message naming the segments due for replacement — and the list was correct. `#norm-caught-disciplines-become-mechanisms` working in its intended direction, doing the next mind's bookkeeping.
    A CLAIMED CONVICTION WAS CHECKED AND FOUND FALSE, then built rather than softened. The incision mask's first draft asserted the crater test would convict it. Removing the mask, the crater test PASSES — on the filled surface a lake cell's receiver sits at the same lake surface, so the unmasked erosion is epsilon-sized. The assertion was corrected and the missing conviction built as a BIT-EXACTNESS test (`a_lake_floor_is_not_quietly_planed_by_the_epsilon_gradient`), because the quantity would hide inside any tolerance: unmasked, 50 of 144 floor cells move in ONE epoch by up to 4.9e-2 m, oriented along the epsilon's flood direction — a metre over a settle history, written into every lake bed, at the largest A on the surface.
    A PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION MISSED AND THE MISS IS RECORDED. Predicted before re-running `discharge_probe`: the epsilon's DIRECTIONAL artefact would persist at reduced amplitude. It persists at INCREASED amplitude (1740 -> 2165 long-run cells, 80% -> 83% inside filled ground), because there is now more filled ground for the reader to orient flow across. What the prediction got right is that restoring the raise does not remove the artefact, since incision is still applied where the epsilon sent the flow. The epsilon's MASS half is retired and convicted; its directional half stands.
    THE REPAIR PROMOTES THE NEXT BLOCKER RATHER THAN PRODUCING THE PHENOMENON, and the honest headline says so. A per-tile read of the rebuilt store finds 8 tiles holding 23 cells (deepest 367 m) against 3 tiles and 12 cells before — small, because the reader shares the carve's contract and, upstream of that, the shipped carve grades each tile to its own perimeter. So this removes the blocker that NO bed could hold water anywhere, and leaves the cross-tile base level (item 1) as the blocker for whether the shipped tiling holds it where geography says it should, and the water timestep (item 4) as the blocker for anything actually standing in it. Three serial causes, two now adjudicated.
  |impact  `erosion.rs`: `fill_depressions` returns the true spill depth; `incise` masks submerged cells; `deposit` traps into the bed with spill-over; `erode` restores the raise before talus and creep. Nomos version `erosion-2026-07-28a-route-filled-incise-bed` (every store stale, world rebuilt, erosion sweep 12.0 s against 12.4 s before — cost is not the obstacle). Nomotheke: the epsilon-fill unphysical term's mass half retired and re-declared directional; a new sign-definite term for trapping efficiency 1. FOUR TESTS, each hand-checked against its own known-bad: `a_crater_survives_the_epoch_that_routes_over_it` (replacing the tripwire, which fired as designed), `an_epoch_over_a_pitted_bed_adds_no_rock`, `a_lake_floor_is_not_quietly_planed_by_the_epsilon_gradient`, `a_lake_silts_up_toward_its_spill_point_and_not_past_it`. NEW SEGMENT `#obs-lakes-are-routed-over-not-carved-away`; `#obs-fill-writes-itself-into-the-bed` DELETED rather than softened — its slug asserted a retired mechanism, and its still-live grain-sweep material was RE-MEASURED under the new law rather than carried across. Present-truth passes with fresh numbers: `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` FE(2)–(8) (trunk gain 3.85x -> 3.25x, assembled seam pits 13.0% -> 19.67%, beacon 60.6% -> 63.5%, arm gap 280 m -> 259/203 m, L9 300-epoch trunk growth 2.4x -> 4.2x, out-degree and run-census rows); `#form-declared-boundary-contract` FE(1)/(4) and Working Notes; `#detail-erosion-composition` steps (2)/(5)/(6) and four residual rows — including two the outside audit caught, the uplift row's stale "strictly positive" (the live driver is signed and near-zero-mean, with a test) and the seam-pit row's missing grain scope; `#obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion` Working Notes carry the chi shift (stage-300 1-R2(sqrt) 0.2882 -> 0.3077, zero-parameter ratio 0.9193 -> 0.9751, recorded residual unmoved at 0.4109 m/epoch against 0.4172) with the attribution marked argued rather than isolated. OUTLINE row replaced; CONSOLIDATION items 1, 1b, 4 and 7 rewritten. NOT CLAIMED: that water now stands in these basins (`#obs-water-fill-never-settles` — 40 s of world time at any level); the L11 and multi-face replications the superseded row had; that the 7% chi shift is caused by retained basins rather than by anything else in the same source tree; any hydrological account of a basin beyond geometric capacity. `bin/check` green. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/src/erosion.rs (fill_depressions/incise/deposit/erode, 4 tests) · crates/vivarium-world/src/nomotheke.rs · core/src/obs-lakes-are-routed-over-not-carved-away.md · core/src/obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins.md · core/src/form-declared-boundary-contract.md · core/src/detail-erosion-composition.md · core/src/obs-chi-shape-is-erosions-criterion.md · core/OUTLINE.md · CONSOLIDATION-STATUS.md · crates/vivarium-world/examples/{base_level_probe,beacon_contract_probe,discharge_probe,chi_convergence_probe}.rs · DECISIONS[the-fill-not-the-tile-edge-is-what-leaves-the-world-without-a-lake] · DECISIONS[the-boundary-contract-becomes-nameable-and-the-beacon-answers-differently-from-the-planet]

|decision[the-same-level-seam-owes-two-objects-and-only-an-exchanged-halo-supplies-either] :date 2026-07-28 :by claude :status proposed :topic architecture
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  DESIGNED AND MEASURED, NOT BUILT — the cross-tile base-level repair is a Jacobi halo exchanged at a declared depth `d` and cadence `sigma`, and the three judgment calls are: (1) the same-level seam owes TWO objects, a boundary DATUM (bed elevation — where downhill is) and a boundary FLUX (discharge and sediment crossing), which fail together and are repaired separately, and a halo supplies only the datum; (2) exchange must be JACOBI (every tile reads one snapshot frozen before any tile advances), because the natural Gauss-Seidel form makes the world a function of build order; (3) the key gains a SCHEDULE DESCRIPTOR `(d, sigma, rho, contract)` and stays O(1) — a tile that depends on its neighbours does NOT drag the planet into its key.
  |reason
    THE CHEAP VERSION WAS MEASURED AND IT IS WORSE THAN DOING NOTHING. One-shot OVERLAP — carve each tile on an (n+2d)^2 window, keep the interior, read no neighbour ever — is the design that costs nothing in the currency this project guards hardest: key stays a pure function of coordinates, no dependency cone, build order cannot matter. On the L13 beacon block (256^2, 4x4 tiles of 64^2, 300 epochs, against the same footprint as ONE field) it moves every column the wrong way at up to 4x the compute: mean |dh| 133–145 m against PLAIN's 118, seam step 1.40–1.94 against the reference's own 1.034, mean elevation 36–77 m off against PLAIN's 36. THE MECHANISM IS THE FINDING: PLAIN's seam step is 0.614, BELOW the reference — an edge-sink tile grades its perimeter flat, so the shipped seam is a small difference between two flattened strips. The shipped seam was QUIET, not small. Overlap removes the flattening (correctly — it was an artefact) and supplies nothing in its place, so the true disagreement becomes visible and the metric overshoots. A repair that makes a metric worse by removing a concealment is the shape worth recording.
    EXCHANGE LANDS ON THE REFERENCE AND CADENCE OUTBUYS DEPTH. Refreshing each tile's halo from the assembled neighbours every sigma epochs puts the seam step at 1.011 (d=8) and 1.040 (d=16) against the reference's 1.034, and mean elevation within 1.8 m (d=32) and 4.8 m (d=16) of it. At equal cost, sigma 50->10 at d=8 moves mean |dh| 110.6->100.3 for NO extra compute, while d 4->32 at sigma=10 moves it 108.2->90.0 for 3.2x the compute. The cheap dial is the frequency; the expensive dial is the depth.
    ORDER-INDEPENDENCE IS AVAILABLE, CHEAP, AND DESTROYED BY THE OBVIOUS IMPLEMENTATION. Forward versus reverse tile sweep, d=8, sigma=10, 50 epochs: Jacobi differs on 0 of 65536 cells; Gauss-Seidel — publishing each tile as produced — differs on 61724 of 65536, mean 130.7 m, max 2289 m, which is the SIZE OF THE SIGNAL BEING REPAIRED. `#form-depend-by-key-never-latest` FE(1) is therefore not a constraint the design must work around but the thing that picks the algorithm. The Gauss-Seidel arm is retained in the probe as the sensitivity control, because a bit-identity assertion nothing could fail is not evidence.
    THE WHOLE-PLANET CLOSURE WAS AN ARTEFACT OF IMAGINING THE KEY AS A PAYLOAD, and this is the crux dissolving rather than being solved. A complete key has NEVER contained its inputs — `query::erosion_key` folds (seed, face, level, oi, oj, nx, epochs) plus each dep's name=version via `Key::with_dep_versions`, and never the content hash of the initial-topography, uplift or climate tiles it consumes, because those coordinates determine those tiles. Under determinism, folding a NAME THAT DETERMINES an input is exactly as complete as folding its bytes. So a halo-dependent tile folds a schedule descriptor, the neighbour set is a function of its own coordinates, the recursion terminates at round zero on the prior, and the payload stays O(1). What IS real is the dependency CONE — one exchange round spreads influence one tile outward, so an isolated tile at round N materializes a radius-N neighbourhood, Theta(N^3) — and it is a SCHEDULING cost, falling on lazy single-tile pulls (the beacon's own access pattern) and not on region sweeps, where every tile-round is shared. `rho`, a declared cone truncation, is the lever that restores laziness, and it is the one parameter with no measurement behind it.
    A CHAOS FLOOR BOUNDS WHAT ANY REPAIR MAY PROMISE, and it kills the attractive "provisional band" formulation. +1 mm on ONE cell 96 cells from a 64^2 core moves that core 13.6 m mean / 395 m max after 300 epochs with only 3.4% of cells bit-identical — a perturbation with no physical content that a halo of any depth would have absorbed. So "the interior beyond width w is final under its own key regardless of neighbours" is FALSE, and no design may promise pointwise agreement. The band is real EARLY and dissolves: boundary influence over local carve magnitude runs 1.03 / 0.83 / 0.50 / 0.25 by ring depth at 50 epochs (a clean decaying band with a final interior behind it) and 1.04 / 0.81 / 0.70 / 0.70 / 1.08 / 1.45 at 300, where the DEEPEST ring is the worst. What arrives is not a wave amplitude that decays but a reorganization of the drainage network, which has no far field. The boundary signal nonetheless stands 13.8x clear of the chaos floor, which is what keeps the defect real and repairable — in the currency of STRUCTURE, since the currency of cell-by-cell identity is available to nobody.
  |impact  TWO NEW SEGMENTS: `#obs-exchange-repairs-the-seam-and-overlap-does-not` (observation/exact, OUTLINE VII Geosphere) carries the measurements so they survive the design's death; `#form-same-level-halo-exchange` (formulation/robust-qualitative, OUTLINE V toolkit, beside the seam law and the face register) carries the design. PRESENT-TRUTH EDITS: `#form-seam-flux-exchange` FE(1) makes tile-meets-tile-at-one-level a first-class seam instance rather than an implicit one, FE(2) separates the boundary DATUM from the flux and says why importing a neighbour's edge elevation is not the state-sharing the clause forbids, and a NEW FE(8) tables the two repair paths so nobody aims the hanging-node register at a same-level base-level problem again (FE(7)'s f32 clause keeps its number — an msc audit cites it); `#form-complete-content-addressed-key` gains FE(6), the descriptor-not-payload law, which is general and was load-bearing here; `#form-declared-boundary-contract` FE(6) names the third contract value `Halo{d, sigma, rho}` and why it is keyable, FE(7) is the old out-of-bounds clause renumbered. TWO NEW INSTRUMENTS, both store-free and world-free and neither touching `erosion.rs` (a concurrent spike owned it): `examples/halo_band_probe` (one core, growing halo, chaos twin, ~11 s) and `examples/halo_exchange_probe` (block arms plus a Jacobi/Gauss-Seidel order-independence gate that ASSERTS, ~145 s). STALE FIGURES CORRECTED in the OUTLINE row for `#obs-tile-outlets-grade-away-the-basins` (3.85x/60.6%/280 m -> 3.25x/63.5%/259 m, matching the segment after the fill repair re-measured it). NOT CLAIMED: one seed, one footprint, one level, one tile grain; the L9 planet regime is unmeasured and expected to differ; the flux half of the repair is untested and the 3.25x starved driver is untouched by anything here; `rho` has no measurement; no keyed caller can select a halo and `EdgeContract` remains outside the key. OPEN AND CHEAP: `#detail-seam-precedents` has no domain-decomposition family, and additive Schwarz is exactly what FE(2) is — a primary read there would supply a convergence theory for d and sigma or convict the analogy. Build verdict FRESH — proposed, not council-sealed.
  |ref crates/vivarium-world/examples/halo_band_probe.rs · crates/vivarium-world/examples/halo_exchange_probe.rs · core/src/obs-exchange-repairs-the-seam-and-overlap-does-not.md · core/src/form-same-level-halo-exchange.md · core/src/form-seam-flux-exchange.md · core/src/form-complete-content-addressed-key.md · core/src/form-declared-boundary-contract.md · core/OUTLINE.md · crates/vivarium-world/src/query.rs (erosion_key, erosion_tile_staged) · crates/vivarium-world/src/store.rs (Key::with_dep_versions) · DECISIONS[the-boundary-contract-becomes-nameable-and-the-beacon-answers-differently-from-the-planet]

|decision[the-eyes-first-verdict-basins-legitimate-circles-fading-and-a-caspian-gap] :date 2026-07-28 :by joseph :status decided :topic physics
  :session b28f9441-4aab-4edf-9d65-3f55c7624558
  THE EYE INSTRUMENT'S FIRST CALIBRATED READING of the rebuilt world (depression paint, L14 region window, F0). Joseph, verbatim: "There are certainly dark areas rendered as ocean that I would expect to be lakes... The ones painted certainly seem like legitimate higher-elevation basins, and the land-masses themselves, while a little noiser than I would expect, are less 'circle+perlin' than they were before, definitely. Also, I see the beginning of real erosion / ridges!" Three verdicts and one detection: (1) painted basin PLACEMENT plausible — the certification no probe could produce; (2) tectonic structure (zonation + signed driver + carve) visibly displacing the craton-circle morphology, residual noise honestly attributed by the HUD to ladder detail; (3) real ridge/erosion structure emerging at carve scale; (4) A NEW GAP DETECTED BY EYE ALONE — dark inland patches rendered as ocean.
  |reason  The detection's mechanism (Claude's analysis, labeled as such): sea is classified by ELEVATION THRESHOLD against the derived datum, not by connectivity — `Fluvial::outlets` and every downstream reader mark any at-or-below-datum cell as sea-connected drain, landlocked or not — so enclosed below-datum basins (the Caspian class, common on real worlds) render as ocean and are EXCLUDED from depression capacity. Joseph's own hypothesis ("too close to sea-level for the painter") was adjacent; the sharper form is that the world cannot currently EXPRESS an endorheic sea. This is the fourth eye-detection of the arc (after the sawtooth anomaly, the seam ribbons, and the no-lakes report that started the day) and the first from the new paint — the instrument the depiction norm exists to keep calibrated, working at spec.
  |impact  `#form-derived-sea-level` Working Notes carry the gap + repair direction (connectivity-aware classification: flood-fill from the true ocean); CONSOLIDATION standing item added; census probe named as the convicting first step. Unmeasured: basin count/sizes in the class.
  |ref core/src/form-derived-sea-level.md · CONSOLIDATION-STATUS.md · changelog/2026-07-28-03-a-pre-registered-prediction-missed-and-that-is-the-practice-working.md


|decision[phase-span-vs-state-is-unadjudicated] :date 2026-07-29 :by claude :status proposed :topic naming
  :session 91ddc84b-489e-4ce1-8898-02d4ad9993ad
  A live conflict in term canon, flagged so it cannot vanish: LEXICON |term[phase] (:status carved) defines a phase as "a span in which a set of macro systems runs until converged," while a competing framing — a phase is a STATE, not an interval — was marked verified in the archived core outline ("the ordinum machinery enforces it and convicted the world Jul 12") and now survives nowhere live. The two readings disagree about what the ordinum's |phase entries denote. AWAITING Joseph: amend the LEXICON entry toward state, reject the state framing, or carve both readings apart. Until adjudicated, cite the LEXICON form.
  |reason
    The candidates file (graduated 2026-07-29) was the only written record of this conflict; graduating it without this entry would have deleted the flag rather than the rubbish. LEXICON is term authority, so the fix is an amendment proposal — not a segment route-around.
